In article <1992Mar28.202729.5495@socrates.umd.edu>, mike@socrates.umd.edu (Mike Santangelo (UNIX/VMS Sysmgr)) writes:

|>I have a VAXstation II with a VCB01 board currently.  Some questions:
|>
|>  1) Is the scrolling in DECwindows handled in hardware on the
|>     VCB01 or by the MicroVAX processor itself?  Kinda slow...
|>     -Observations: I pulled the VCB01 out and noticed it had
|>      an old Motorola 6545 display processor, wow!  I remember
|>      that chip...  Was their low-end chip at the time.
|>     -There was an AMD chip to the left of it, anyone know what it
|>      is?  Is it a small processor?
|>     -Does this board have any local intelligence aside from the 6545?

The VCB01 is essentially a single plane frame buffer.  The host processor
(in your case a MicroVAX II) is responsible for all drawing, scrolling and
BLT moves.  


|>  2) How drastically different is the VCB02 board(-set)?  Besides having
|>     multiple planes, does it have any local intelligence that might
|>     help to offload the MicroVAX  SPU from anything when, say, running
|>     DECwindows?
|>     -Will it drive a VR260 monitor?  How many planes do I get with
|>      one board?  How does having multiple 4-bit-plane boards affect
|>      operation with a VR260?

The VCB02 is the first graphics accelerator that Digital used in its 
workstation lines.  It is made up of two or three quad Q-bus boards.

The first board is the graphics processor board, which has the system 
interface, and the graphics acceleration.  The second and third board
are 4-plane frame buffers.  (the option was available as both a 4 and 8 plane
configuration)

It will drive any of the 1024x864 @60Hz monitors that Digital has sold, however
I don't believe it was ever "qualified" (like FCC testing) with the newer
monitors like the VR297 (16" Trinitron)


