>On the other hand, one could say that crashme is the moral equivalent of
>jabbing a screwdriver into the innards of your Walkman and then
>complaining that it wasn't built sturdily enough.

I disagree.  I thought we had kernel state and user state to prevent the
users from constructing said screwdriver.

Perhaps the hardware isn't doing enough.  And possibly the software, though
I think we're placing too many eggs in the wrong (software) basket these
days.

>
>I don't want to appear to be defending unrobustness -- certainly we're
>working on hardening against the problems that crashme turns up and a
>multiuser system does have to protect itself against malicious behavior.

Or simply disorderly behavior caused by accident.  Face it - we all like
to play dispatcher and create arrays of pointers to functions.  One small
slip makes that kinda program the moral equivalent of crashme.

>
>On the other hand, system vendors have limited resources and have to
>choose whether to spend them on things like defending against deliberate
>attempts to tickle the hardware into failing or on things like adding
>functionality and improving performance.  Since *most* sites are peopled
>by cooperative users who *don't* spend their time trying to crash the
>system, this is not as simple a choice as it might appear.

I think these problems need to be solved at a different level than they're
being approached now.  Perhaps once the current mips-o-mania subsides
(who said the speed of light was a bad thing?) we can concentrate on some
more interesting issues.  Like creating reliable software and hardware
systems that have more than an elemental view of "robust".

Perhaps it's time for a new book: The hardware that can say no.

Of course, I lament the demise of the iAPX 432, so that puts me in poor
stead with the bulk of current thought on architecture.  The nice thing
about computers is that events move fast enough so that good ideas can
get a second chance before their authors pass on.

