In article <walterg.701837376@aupair.cs.athabascau.ca> walterg@aupair.cs.athaba
scau.ca (Walter Gray) writes:


>>   We recently upgraded a DEC3100 to the 5000/125 and have found the 
>>   same slooow screen update. I will follow the HX upgrade info.

>>   I would howver like to know what SCSI disks in the range of 600MByte
>>   to 1.2 GByte have been found to fit in the expansion chassis and to 
>>   work reliably.

 I gather from your note that you've already considered the RZ56, RZ57 and
the RZ58 disks from DEC (there is a new breakdown on the DEC RZ series disks
and performances available now    gatekeeper:pub/DEC/TriAdd/scsi-cnfg.ps
This document was prepared by the DEC storage group and goes over all of the
DEC RZ products and performances.

 As for third party disks, I think you'll find that Maxtor, Micropolis,Seagate,
HP, Fujitsu and Hitachi all have products in that range. You'll want to ask
them about DEC compatible firmware (most of the volume disk suppliers keep a
variant of firmware for each of the major system vendors). We've not worked
much with the HP or Fujitsu stuff in our group, and only a limited amount with
the Hitachi disks.

 The major compatibility issue (if there is one) is likely to be the firmware
implementation. Examples of this are:

	* Not responding to a SCSI bus reset within 250ms. The SCSI-1 spec did
 	  not specify a time and some vendors who are moving their SCSI-1 disk
	  products to SCSI-2 interfaces didn't catch the 250ms limit in the
	  SCSI-2 specification.

	* Returning a mode page 5, size 0. This disk vendor had this item in
	  their firmware to "grandfather" a problem from another system house.
	  However, mode page 5 is for floppies, and a size 0 causes the disk
	  to fail as an Ultrix installation device.

	* Hard strapping a synchronous mode instead of allowing synchronous
	  to be negotiated between Host and Target.

 That doesn't mean there will be a problem, just that sometimes implementation
of firmware can be an issue. If the company has a DEC compatible firmware you
probably won't see any of these issues.

>>   Thanks.

 You're quite welcome.

