Today I just got a whole bunch of nice error messages of the form
 
inews: Cannot open /usr/local/lib/news/active (r+): Permission denied
 
Doing an 'ls -lg' of /usr/local/bin/inews (NNTP's mini-inews) and
/usr/local/lib/news/inews (BNEWS's real inews) and /usr/local/bin/rnews
(BNEWS's news spooler), I get:
 
-rwxr-xr-x  1 usenet   news       122880 Mar 28 16:34 /usr/local/bin/inews*
-rwxr-xr-x  1 news     news       204800 Mar 28 16:35 /usr/local/lib/news/inews*
-rwxr-xr-x  1 news     news       204800 Oct 31 13:45 /usr/local/bin/rnews*
 
Setuid-ness is no longer.  So I go "Great.  Not again"  Su to root.  Reset
the suid bit.  But,
 
picard [60] chmod u+s /usr/local/bin/inews
chmod: can't change /usr/local/bin/inews: Permission denied
picard [60]  kljfoiewjvwieeu {Sysadmin promptly pulls gun out of drawer and
                              shoots himself in head.  Head falls on keyboard.}
 
 
This is the fifth or sixth time I've had to do the following workaround,
all because UCX probably has its idea of user-ids and proxies screwed up.
 
cd /usr/local/bin
mv inews Inews
cp Inews inews
chown usenet.news inews
chmod u+s inews
rm -f Inews
 
 
According to our VAX sysadmin, there are indeed proxies for 'root', 'daemon',
'news', 'usenet', and all other "normal" user accounts.  I even watched over
his shoulder as he re-entered all the proxies from a copy of /etc/passwd
I had just printed out.
 
SO WHAT'S THE PROBLEM???!?!?!?
 
AND WHAT CAN BE DONE ABOUT IT???!?!?
 
