	Any Ideas for good April 1st jokes for DEC based systems?
	One example we did follows:

	DEC's IAS operating system had a very neat feature, a command of
	"SET/CLI=..." which let you put any program you wanted up as the
	command line interperter. Made security easier, canned applications
	could never bomb out and give a person MCR or DCL, etc, but it did open
	the world for hacking. One May 1 we introduced a slight variation of MCR
	for one of our users...

	1/ Normal MCR had a 16 bit word associated with each user name, each
	   bit of which granted a privilege, ie bit 2 meant you could do file
	   operations, (copy, create, delete, etc), bit 3 meant you could use
	   editors, etc.) In general, higher order bits turned on meant more
	   and more privileges, so 177777 was a super user, 000017 was quite
	   restricted.

	2/ We rewrote MCR for one user, (whose normal privilege word was 177777
	   or anything goes,) so that it worked normally, until he made some
	   error in his command line, (such as saying DIT instead of DIR.) The
	   program would then lop off the highest bit of privilege. On sub-
	   sequent errors, further bits would be turned off.

	3/ Pretty soon, he started getting error messages of the form;
	   "Current user privileges do not allow that function!" of course,
