Setting aside the obviously assinine accusations, I see a cry here for
more coverage of publicly-available software in the trade press.

Speaking for myself and my experience at UNIX Today! and SCO Magazine,
I can hardly say there is any deliberate effort to bury free software.
The plain, boring truth is that freeware doesn't have a marketing
department behind it, there are no public relations people pushing it,
sending out press releases, holding announcements, etc. 

Reporters here and at other trade publications get literally hundreds
of announcements a week, all of them from companies with some new
utility, software or hardware kit that is interesting and will be of
interest to at least a percentage of our readers. We can only write so
much, and we try to spend that time writing about products we
feel will be useful to the largest number of our readers.

True, there is a wealth of free software out there and a lot of it is
good stuff. I've written about quite a lot of it ... well, let me take
that back. I've written about the eight or nine free packages we use
in our office in my column over the last couple of years (see
Sourcerer's Apprentice in OST), including ELM, CNEWS, rn, NNTP, smail,
and UUPC. I even recall at one point writing that smail is something
you can't live without if you're on an SCO Xenix box and want to do
*real* e-mail because Xenix doesn't have an MTA. 

There has been other coverage in our pages as well, SCO Magazine
recently published a full-length feature article on freeware, but I'm
speaking only of what I know from having written it myself.

Like all publications, OST and SCO Magazine honestly strive to serve
our readers. If you feel a particular area of coverage is lacking, or
if you know of something you feel warrants coverage, by all means, get
involved. Pick up the phone, or send e-mail, and tell us what you want
covered.

