In article <1992Mar19.150237.7309@crpmks.uucp>, billb@crpmks.uucp (Bill Bochnik (Info Systems)) writes:
> In article <203558@unix.cis.pitt.edu> sillett@edc3jr.gsph.pitt.edu (Robert L. Sillett) writes:
> >
> >  [ asking about relative merits of vax 6000 vs uvax 3100 ]
> 
> This has been a great subject of debate here.  We had people saying that they 
> had these great workstations that had the same compute power as our bigger 
> vax's.  This mat be true, but they are strictly single user systems (although 
> you can have more that I person on at a time).  Putting many users on a high 
> end workstation dogs the machine (the bus cannot handle the load).  The 
> microVax is similar.  You can put many people on it, but you will not get the 
> performance of the larger machines.  The bus is slower, and clustering is 
> slower as you would probably use ethernet rather than CI as Digital does not 
> market a Q bus Ci adapter.  I do not know your specific app. so I can't help 
> you out any more than this.  Food for thought.
> 
  [ I am on the ULTRIX side of the fence, so my choices are not quite comp-
    arable to those facing VMS folks ]

  While I sort of agree with the above sentiments, I also think that they over-
  look one big factor: budgets.  Yes, it would be nice to have the option to 
  plunk down the cash for a VAX 6000/6nn machine, HSC controller, the disks it
  can interface with, etc.  But for may of us, that is not an option, so we 
  have to come up with more creative solutions that are as cost-effective as 
  possible for the organizations we work for.

  As the above notes, it's possible to buy cpu power for relatively low cost.
  The variance lies largely in i/o capabilities.  I believe that many of us
  have been able to overcome the deficiencies in the latter, at least to the
  extent that we  1) are able to make use of much of the potential cpu power
  in our "little" machines and 2), get our work done.

  Our core "system" is composed of a small set of DECsystem 5000/200's.  The 
  total cost of the configuration is less than half of what we consider to be
  a roughly equivalent VAX 6000 configuration in terms of overall capability
  (yep, that is our subjective assessment).  If we take into account mainten-
  ance costs, say over a three to four year period, the difference is even 
  greater.

  Several things we did to obtain the throughput we required:

  1. invest in multiple SCSI interfaces where needed.  As others have pointed
     out, the SCSI interface may lack in the necessary throughput.  And, in
     fact, when we ran various i/o tests involving 2 or more disks attached
     to a single SCSI interface on one of our systems, we could see this very
     readily.  But for an additional $1700 or so list price per unit, one can
     add additional SCSI interfaces, limit the number of disks attached to 
     each one, thereby boosting i/o throughput very substantially.  One can 
     buy a rather large number of these for less than the price of a single HSC;

  2. use a larger number of "small" (third-party) disks and spread data/files
     among them.  Our "standard" disk is an HP 600 MB unit, apx. $1600 with
     power supply, cable, and 5-year warranty.  For many applications, the
     primary bottleneck is the need to serve up bits of data from many files
     "concurrently"; multiple spindles do this more effectively than a smaller
     number of large RA series disks;

  3. use a lot of cheap (third party) memory and (in the case of ULTRIX, 
     anyway) configure as large a disk buffering facility as your memory will
     support.  This has limitations, as DEC warns in its manuals.  But in our
     environment, putting 56 to 96 MB on each system and reserving 30-50% of
     that to disk buffers has produced outstanding results.  Cost: less than
     $90/MB.

     The clearest indication we had of the effect of this occurred on a system
     that we use for what is essentially a text database application: we 
     upgraded to the next rev of ULTRIX and, in the process, unintentionally
     reduced memory set aside for disk buffers to apx. 5.6 MB (e.g., reset to
     the 10% default value).  With 25 users and some system-to-remote system 
     activity, the machine began to bog down and the load average climbed to
     4.5 or so.  Increasing disk buffers to 25 MB brought the load average 
     down to less than one and throughput increased to close to "instantaneous"
     response;

  4. distribute functions among systems.  We have a dedicated database server,
     a dedicated mail system, and three "user login" systems from which people
     run front ends to the database and mail and also run locally-stored 
     packages (such as SAS).

     Each machine is configured for its specific function(s) and can be
     upgraded independently as necessary, for relatively low cost.  Cross-
     machine file access is via NFS which is flawed but hey, it works .... most
     of the time....  But in general, disks are isolated, where possible, to
     the system that "needs" them.  For instance, the database server has 8
     disks "dedicated" to it.

  With this setup we can put a reasonable number of people on what are essen-
  tially workstations without "dogging" the machines. In our case, the limit
  appears to be apx. 40-45 concurrent users on a single login system for decent
  response time/throughput.  Activities include database retrieval/updating/
  report generation, a LOT of electronic mail, document retrieval and transfer
  to remote sites via dial-up, and a typical mix of text-editing, some WP, 
  etc.  To put this in another way: having spent a fair amount of time on VAX
  systems running ULTRIX and on VAX-based clusters as a user, running similar
  applications, my own personal observation is that we are not losing anything
  performance- or throughput-wise.
  
  
  ... but yes, one "big" system that could accomplish the same thing would be
      very nice.

   greg pavlov,
   pavlov@karloff.fstrf.org


