All that you said in favour of UNIX might possibly be true (allthough I
strongly doubt the propriety of using the word "standard" in connection
with UNIX - an OS that doesn't exist but in a mess of different
dialects) , however, there still remains a main advantage of DOS (or the
Mac OS) and that's cost. 
Most suppliers of applications for UNIX-based machines charge ungodly
amounts for software that doesn't even have a proper user interface and
requires months to only get marginally acquainted with. On top of that
the pricing is often based on the MIPS of the machine the software 
is destined to run on - a policy that is outright revolting (Didn't I already 
pay for the machine?).
And last but not least there's copy protection. Few DOS applications are
copy protected now. Users can share the software, try things out before
purchase and so on. That's illegal and disrepectful of the programmers'
efforts to be sure, but - like it or not - it is a major factor in
deciding what kind of a machine to buy. 

As long as UNIX doesn't offer the same kind of crude plug and play that
DOS offers due to its primitivity and as long as Software suppliers
don't offer software that is comparable in ease of use to DOS (or to set
the standards higher Mac OS) applications I don't see how UNIX will ever
get anywhere in the low end market.

