Bzzz!  Wrongo!  DOS, just like any computer system, requires that
time be spent to administer the system.  As you say, frequently
DOS does not require a *separate* person.  But if you make your
secretary maintain his/her own system, the maintainence of that
system is going to suffer (usually).

I FREQUENTLY hear (as I'm sure everybody has) longish horror stories
about how hard it was to install something or other.  It may be
just as simple as installing an upgrade to an application, but
frequently Something Goes Wrong and the novices (heck, even the
experts sometimes) get lost on tangents and spend days (sometimes)
doing the job.

This seems to be true on any system.  My theory is that it's worse
on DOS or MacIntosh because the people maintaining them tend to be
novices.  Another problem is that the design of the systems does not
include any insulation between processes, so much too frequently
they step on one another.

You want an example?  Go read any of Jerry Pournelle's columns in
BLYTE Magazine!  The man is not an idiot and he's been dealing with
computers for a long long time.  But it seems that every single
month he comes up with another DOOZY of an installation problem.
Generally the problems he has is with getting different products
to work together; he comes up with a solution which amounts to
taking away some bits of bailing wire and installing new wire, and
never comes to the realization that the ROOT problem is the DESIGN
of that system.  And in the meantime he helps sell people on the
idea that DOS is a reasonable usable system (though with flaws).

> whereas UNIX will require a knowledgeable 
>person to spend some time with it. 

Sigh..

>The only company I see making any 
>strides at all in this direction is SUN with it's SOLARIS effort. The ability
>to run DOS applications will be critical to it's success - like it or not, 
>there are just too many DOS users and too many DOS apps to ignore.

Granted DOS capability will help in the transition to a better
designed platform.  There are other companies working on making
system administration easier.  You been keeping track of OSF and
the distributed management environment?  I think there's other
work going on besides OSF/Tivoli for that matter..



