In article <1992Mar18.142339.5578@csusac.csus.edu> croft@csusac.csus.edu (Steve Croft) writes:



>> It's nice that clock speeds and CPU performance are being kicked up,
>> but I would sure like to see the same degree of improvement in storage
>> systems.  Having these souped-up CPUs with current storage technology
>> is like driving your 'Vette in first gear...

>> steve

Steve,

  You're right. As Marcus told you I/O is a real watchword in the industry
now. Seems like all of a sudden folks have figured out that having a real
performance engine is only useful if you can get fuel to it. If you can't
get enough fuel to the engine it stalls. If you can't get enough data to a
CPU it also stalls.

  That was one of the reasons why when we were designing the DS5000/200
back in 1989 we decided not to use any of the I/O protocols that were in
the commercial market at the time. And, that's why Mike Nielsen invented
the TURBOchannel. He had no intention of designing another system bus with
a lot of additional baggage, just a clean, simple, fast I/O solution. It's
also why we specified twice the power of most other buses and provided for
cooling air as well. All in a package that's applicable to the desktop.

  If you're interested in higher storage throughput, I recommend that you
talk to GENROCO about their next generation IPI RAID-3 implementation. It's
got real high end capability for volume storage with high data throughput
as well (their current IPI controller will handle disk drives up to 12MB/s).
As a matter of fact, we're having to work on file system improvements to be
able to take advantage of the improvements in storage performance.

  We've also got internal efforts focused on SCSI RAID developments and still
more third party providers who are working on FAST DIFFERENTIAL SCSI.

  And in the network arena, we've been shipping FDDI on TURBOchannel since
January of 1990. But, it's not stopping there, the first SCI (Scalable Coheren
Interface) design for TURBOchannel was done at the University of Oslo in 
Norway. There is already a third party option being completed for ATM 
(Asynchronous Transfer Mode, an emerging TeleComm standard). ISDN hardware 
already exists on the PDS5000/2x and there are folks working on HiPPI to 
TURBOchannel.

  So, we're not standing still. And, in preparation for your future needs
as well, the first TIG (TURBOchannel Industry Group) session discussions of
the next generations of TURBOchannel (speed selectable, 200MB/s) are started. 
And with an implementation that will provide complete backwards compatibility 
for all TURBOchannel options that comply to the specifications today. You can
find information on this on   gatekeeper:pub/DEC/TriAdd  for your enjoyment.

  I'm sorry that rambled on so long. But, I hope that gives you a better feel
for the fact that we are working on improvements.

				Thanx,
				  AJ


