Well, monday evening I helped someone configure the printer on their Macintosh.

I mean, let's forget about IBM. The Mac is supposed to be even easier and more
convenient. My old 128K Mac is certainly very easy to use.

Let me tell you, "System Administration" under System 7 is not a job for
amateurs. I'm a Mac novice, but a professional programmer with experience
in dozens of environments, and there were times I was stumped. Who would
imagine that there would be a whole menu of options hidden under a radio
button with no graphic indication it was any different from the ones next
to it? Who would imagine that opening a document and closing it would leave
the application running in memory? And what happened to the old Mac technique
of saving hunks on disk to make room for new work? I think Apple has blown
it in a big way.

But, still, I didn't have to edit any text files, I didn't have to run
separate configuration programs for separate applications, and so on, like
you do under DOS. Anyone who tells you they don't need a system administrator
IS a system administrator.
