>time booting is spent in FSCK (on ULTRIX systems).  Does anyone have
>or know of a faster fsck ?

	Fsck does its thing at its own good pace, and there's not much
you can do about it. If you've got multiple disks/controllers, you can
interleave the fscks by being careful about the pass numbers in fstab,
or use the "preen" program, which is a little smarter about interleaving
the fsck passes. Generally, though, fsck is necessary, and it's slow
by necessity, since it has to look all over your disk.
	Another option is to not use filesystems, or mount things read
only. (for example, don't have fstab entries that imply your CDROMS are
to be checked, or fsck will blissfully check them)

mjr.
