pavlov@niktow.canisius.edu (Greg Pavlov) writes:

>  ... right.  Especially the BIG assumption that you can keep that LOOONNNG
>  pipeline going. 

	Alpha does some clever stuff to keep the pipeline going. Apparently
instead of doing like some architectures and having (say) an exception flag
that gets raised by the floating point multiply if there's an error (and then
having to flush the pipeline) Alpha has several floating point multiplies:
multiply-and-truncate-on-overflow, multiply-and-give-an-error, etc, etc. I
am not real familiar with the details but it's a pretty clever approach.
If you know what you want the chip to do, it'll Do The Right Thing and you
don't have to flush the pipeline (unless that's what doing the right thing
is for your application)   Hmmm.... I *think* it's ok to talk about this
stuff here. ;) If I disappear from the net, someone call the cops. ;)

	The 100+Mflops I mentioned earlier is based on measurements using
standard floating point benchmarks.

