> only. (for example, don't have fstab entries that imply your CDROMS are
> to be checked, or fsck will blissfully check them)

Another option is to use a fast-restart filesystem, such as log-based
filesystems, of which JFS (on AIX) is currently the most widely-used.
Others are also available.  For a recent paper, see:

@INPROCEEDINGS{Rosenblum91,
    AUTHOR = "Mendel Rosenblum and John K. Ousterhout",
    TITLE = "The design and implementation of a log-structured file system",
    BOOKTITLE = "Proceedings of 13th ACM Symposium on Operating Systems Principles",
    CONFLOCATION = "Asilomar, Pacific Grove, CA ",
    PUBLISHER = "Association for Computing Machinery SIGOPS",
    MONTH = "October",
    YEAR = "1991",
    PAGES = "1--15",
    KEY = "Rosenblum91"}

The time-to-fsck becomes an issue when you have 10s of GB of disk 
hanging off a machine.  It becomes a real problem once you get
near 100 GB.

