In article <712@niktow.canisius.edu> pavlov@niktow.canisius.edu (Greg Pavlov) writes:
>  200 Mhz on the Alpha is very very impressive, but each of those single-phase
>  "Mhz" doesn't quite have the same effect that a "Mhz" does on a PA-RISC or
>  MIPS Rnnnn chip, no ? Or am I missing something ?

What does single-phase or multiphase have do with it ? I've seen that
before from so-called analysts that didn't know what they were talking about.
There are different ways of getting things done with clocks, what people
*really* mean when they bring this up are the pipeline latencies, that
is the time between when an operation starts and you are allowed to make
use of it. Pipeline length does have a influence, but it doesn't tell
the whole story. The R4000 has a 8-stage integer pipeline, the Alpha
only has 7 stages. The HP machines I believe use a 5 stage pipeline with
the same structure as the R3000 pipeline (all that is in the integer unit).

Now, neither the HP nor the Rx000 machines have pipelined floating point
units, trading in throughput for latency. That is a win if you have lots
of dependencies. I don't have the R4000 latencies handy and know that
the HP ones are ungodly short, for the 21064 it's 6 cycles, no matter
whether it's add/mul/sub or single/double.  Now, compare apples to apples,
let's see the latency in nanoseconds (10ns HP 7100, 5ns 21064):

	21064	7100
	  30	 20
	  
Suddenly doesn't look that much worse. Especially considering that
for vectorizable codes you're going to take advantage of that added
floating point bandwidth. Also some of the latencies look quite a
bit different in the integer pipeline once you look at this in actual
timing (this whole point becomes moot if HP manages to make the existing
pipeline design run at 5nS in the next round. I wouldn't bet my car on
it, both MIPS and Digital had to eventually user longer pipelines. Also,
the ALU's in the R4000 and 21064 are 64bit machines, not 32 as the HP one,
tough luck addressing the next round of disc drives that cross the 2 Gbyte
barrier).

In defense of the R4000:
The R4000 people have real machines on peoples desks (SGI Crimson, while
both the 71000 and 21064 are *chip* announcements, not quite fair).



