Since exceptions are not reported precisely (unless you use TRAPB), the issue
of exceptions interfering with keeping the pipeline going are greatly reduced,
I would think. Also, if one considers long multiply-add loops or such things,
I suspect the integer and branch code to keep the array indexing and looping
going will overlap nicely with the FP work, so one might really obtain peak
performance for long bursts (given all cache hits, or a good enough memory
subsystem). Of course, Amdahl's Law applies concerning total execution time:
other, non-FP intensive code could drag a program as a whole down quite a bit,
even if you maintain top speed in the inner loops. Still, I would think the
Alpha design and 21064 implementation have great potential for high flops.
