I wish to thank the people who responded to my post. I enclose below the
replies I received. (I apologize to one other good samaritan, whose
name I cannot recall, for losing his message.)

After I posted the question, I managed to experiment more and got the
whole thing to work. I do not claim complete correctness in my approach,
and would be glad to receive your comments about it.


********************* Begin My Hack *******************************


         Installing Ultrix from the CD ROM to a 3rd party Drive.
         -------------------------------------------------------


1. When you first boot from the CD ROM choose the sys management
option. This will put you in a shell with not many tools. However,
"cd" will be available.

2. cd to /dev and "MAKEDEV rz0" or whatever the disk is called by
your ultrix system (You can find this out in the monitor by
executing a cnfg <slot-number> or some such thing. My slot number was 3.)

3. For each of the partitions you want, run "mkfs /dev/rrz0<partition>" with
number of sectors, blksize, etc all put in. For example, for partition 'a'
you would use "mkfs /dev/rrz0a" with the options. The man page for
mkfs lists the options. (Note for confused beginners like me: The rz0a
device refers to a character device and the rrz0a refers to a block device).

4. cd to /etc and run chpt with the right partition sizes.

5. Now exit the shell with a cntrl-d and continue with the installation.

6. Build the part of the kernel after which you will be asked to boot again
from your Hard Disk Drive. 

7. Go thru the steps and when it comes to the part where it asks you for
the /usr partitions, type a control-c and edit the disktab file with
all the info that is necessary. A sample disktab entry for various 
3rd party Drives is available for anonymous ftp at gatekeeper.dec.com.
Run "newfs -v /dev/rrz0<partition> rzxx" for each partition.

8. Type Cntrl-d and continue with installation. You should be fine now.

***************************** End of My Hack *****************************


