Have you tried forgetting about the disktab entries? Usually, the
default values will be taken from the driver. I've added below a
posting I made to an internal (DEC) notesfile where I changed the
partitions on a system disk. I hope it'll be of some help and at least
give you some ideas.

Good Luck!

Dave

----------------------------------------------------------------------------

Below is edited highlights from the 'Backup & Restore' manual, Section 3.6.

3.6 Local Restoration of the root and /usr file system.

This section assumes a dump of the root and /usr file systems is available. 

1. Boot the TK50 (or CD-ROM) device where the standalone system volume is mounted.

After the boot command, the system prompts you to enter 1 for the basic
installation, 2 for the advanced installation or 3 for system management.

2. Enter 3 for system management

3. Run MAKEDEV in the /dev directory to make the system disk special
file and the tape device special file:

# cd /dev
#MAKEDEV 'mnemonic'

Device	Mnemonic
rdisks	rz		SCSI disks
tapes	tz*		SCSI tapes

where 'mnemonic' refers to the device mnemonic.

4. Change to the root directory

# cd /

5. Make a new root file system using the mkfs command

# mkfs 'dev name' 32768 'sector' 'tracks'

The 'dev name' variable specifies the raw device special file.
'sectors' and 'tracks' is device specific.


Disk	Sectors	Tracks
ra60	42	4
ra70	33	11
ra80	31	14
ra81	51	14
ra81	57	15
ra90	69	13
rd53	18	8
rd54	17	15
rm05	32	19
rp07	50	32
rf30	37	6
rf31	50	8
rf71	37	16
rz55	36	15
rz56	54	15


eg for disk partition 'a' of an rd54 disk on a VAXstation 2000

# /etc/mkfs /dev/rra0a 32768 17 15

6. Reload the boot block - ONLY NEEDED IF THERE IS PHYSICAL DEVICE DAMAGE

# dd if-/vaxboot of=device conv=sync

7. Check the new file system. THIS MUST BE DONE.

# /etc/fsck device

8. Mount the root file system

# /etc/mount device /mnt

9. Change to the /mnt directory and restore the root file system

# cd /mnt
# restore -rf device

If restoring from the default tape, the f option and device are not needed

10. Unmount the file system and check for consistency

# cd /
# /etc/umount device
# /etc/fsck device

11. Halt and reboot as follows

# sync
# sync
# halt



>>>b device

----------------------------------------------------------------------------
>From the NOTES file:

Here's how  I  changed the partitions on my system disk....it may be obvious
to  ultrix  gurus,  but I haven't seen it written down anywhere for relative
novices.  The  basic  reference  manual which gives a generic explanation is
"Guide to Backup and Restore" pp 3-11 to 3-13.

My system  is a DECstation 5000/200 PXG w/32M memory, RZ55 system disc, RZ56
user disc.  You'll  have  to  change  the  device  mnemonics  for  different
devices/systems.

/* Get into single user mode */

$ shutdown now

/* Back up the system disc for all partitions currently in use:
** here I just needed to back up the root partition, 'a'.
*/

# dump 0u /

/* Check the current partition sizes: specify the partition containing the
** super block of the raw device.
*/

# chpt -q /dev/rrz0a

/dev/rrz0a
Current partition table:
partition     bottom        top       size    overlap
    a              0      32767      32768    c,h
    b          32768     163839     131072    c
    c              0     649039     649040    a,b,d,e,f,g,h
    d         163840     316285     152446    c,g
    e         316286     468731     152446    c,g
    f         468732     649039     180308    c,g
    g         163840     649039     485200    c,d,e,f
    h              0          0          0    a,c

/* Decide  what size you want the partitions: I wanted 'a' to be 48M long so
** that it wouldn't be 92% full at 16M (size=32768 blocks). So I changed 'a'
** and 'b' as described in note 189.2 to be:
**
**    a              0      98303      98304    c,h
**    b          98304     163839      65536    c
**
** Do this by:
*/

# chpt -v -pa 0 98304 -pb 98304 65536 /dev/rz0a
# newfs /dev/rrz0a      /* makes a new file system on the system device */
# halt                  /* get into console mode */

/* Now  boot off your installation tape and when asked for what installation
** you want, choose "3) System Management". Then type in the following:
*/

# cd /dev               /* change to the devices directory */

/* Make some devices for the system to know about */

# MAKEDEV tz5           /* tape unit at SCSI address 5 */
# MAKEDEV rz0           /* disc at SCSI address 0 */
# cd /                  /* change back to the root directory */

/* This  line makes a rootfile system on the system device: 98304 is the 48M
** space  needed  for the root system. 36 and 15 come from the table on page
** 3-12  and  are  device  specific  for  rz0  (RZ55).  This  line  probably
** over-writes what was done above in newfs, but I'm taking no chances!
*/

# mkfs /dev/rrz0a 98304 36 15   /* use the RAW device name here */

/* Now load in the saved system  files for the root file system */

# /etc/fsck /dev/rz0a   /* check the new file system. This line IS needed */
# /etc/mount /dev/rz0a /mnt
# cd /mnt
# restore -rf /dev/rmt0h        /* read from the tape unit */
# cd /
# /etc/umount /dev/rz0a
# /etc/fsck /dev/rz0a
# sync
# sync
# halt
# boot          /* uses the console bootpath */

And presto! you have a working system again. Easy wasn't it??!!

Dave

