One of the interesting aspects of this whole debate is the convergence and
integration of these "factions".  I was working for CCI when they produced
the first 16 mip mini-computer, and when you would hang 75 users on a single
VAX 11/780 running 1 MIPS.  Today, a 386 PC, can actually outperform that
386.  On the other hand, OpenWindows chews up 90% of a sparc2.  Finding the
best "blend" of hardware and software is more interesting.  If you want
a faster Sparc, just use the Xsun server from MIT.

We are also seeing more "teamwork" between PC's, UNIX, and Mainframes.
PC's running windowing terminal emulators can reduce the overhead of
the Unix and Mainframe.  Remote Procedure Calls can further reduce
the presentation overhead.

There is that final "gotcha" that may put UNIX, in the form of something
like Coherant, ahead in the running.  When the day is done, many people
still go home and work on their PC/XT with the 20 meg drive.  Come up
with a "Baby X", "TCP/IP", and "SLIP" in a kernal that runs, even slowly
on a PC/AT with 1-2 meg of RAM, 10 Meg of disk, and Hercules Monocrome
monitor, with optional VGA, EGA, or even CGA drivers, and you have
broken the Microsoft hold.

The other thing that is interesting in the Fortune 500 mentality, it that
they would rather pay $100/copy of software they can get off the internet
or a BBS than to download it for free.  The problem is that the time
required to port, configure, build and install even the basic MIT X11R4
release becomes expensive when considering that they have to pay one person
$35/hour for 2 weeks to do the port.  Add to that, the 10 people who
want it yesterday and seem unable to function until the utility is
installed, and you can be talking a price of $1000/copy of "free"
software.

One of the blessings of open software is a nearly unlimited number of options.
Unfortunately, when you first install X11, everybody goes option happy.  I have
heard of corporations mandating standard color schemes and options just so
people will quit playing with the toys.  The irony is that once they do
find comfortable personalised settings, they become vicously posessive, even
refusing hardware and software upgrades for fear of losing their custom
tweaked system.  When SUN made the switch from Sunview to OpenWindows,
people resisted until we converted their backgrounds to xbitmaps so that
they could have their favorite poster in the background.

The bottom line is that computer decisions are often made on a very subjective
basis.  People will pick "notepad" over GNU Emacs because it's so simple, never
being concerned with the power they are missing.  Put glitzy menu bars,
buttons, color options, and "help keys", along with correct interpretation
of all the labled keys on their keyboard and they won't give it up.

Possibly the most unpredictible factor in this who debate is what Richard
Stallman called the "Customer Service Consultants".  These people take
products like GNU, X11, and other "Free" software, configure it so that
costomers can us it with no special training, and create little configuration
generators that simply modify the configuration databases.  They put
the source code up on a server somewhere and the customer pays very
generously.  Of course, the same configurations can be sold to several
customers.  Often, these consultants contribute back to "Freeware"
products, knowing that it will be yet another market.


Accept the personalities and the "realities" which have so little
to do with the engineering.  Motif, OpenLook, Windows NT, OS/2,
and Athena/MIT aren't really all that different in terms of
engineering.  It's the marketing that makes the difference.

As to the "religion of UNIX", there have been several UNIX workalikes,
some of which have superceded each other.  BSD 4.1 & 4.2 bear very
little resemblance to the original version 6 & 7 AT&T UNIX, which
was more like Coherant of today.  SysV picked up many distinctions
from Xenix, Venix, OS/9, OS/2, VRTX, and even MS/DOS.  X11 was
radically influenced by MacIntosh, Amiga, and GEM/Atari.  Mach
took many of the Distinctions of MS/DOS and OS/2 to come up
with the Driver as Task concepts.  OSF even inherited some of
the traits of MVS and VM.  The technologies are converging while
creating new distinctions which provide a competitive edge.

People will continue to invent cars that run on acetone and water,
cars that go 200 MPH, and cars that are powered by motercycle
engines.  Once blended with the basic "GM standard car", you
get the spectrum ranging from the Honda Civic to the Lincoln
Towne Car Stretch Limo.  You may have trouble switching from
automatic to 5-speed, but in general, the cars operate in
a somewhat standard way.  Over time, computers have been
working toward that same type of uniform interface, at both
the application and the programming level.  This is simlar
to keeping the principles similar for the mechanic as well
as the driver.

A magazine which covers that convergence and diversity is a
good idea.  There is a big difference between the diversity
in the days of the AppleII vs Commodore 64 vs TRS-80 vs
COCO vs IBM, where, ironically, about the only comon thread
was the Basic in ROM written by a young student who has now
grown up and wants to join the Unix/POSIX party with Windows NT.

It would be nice to see vendors advertizing "integration packages"
such as "GNU", Linux, Mach, BSD386, X11, and TCP/IP in various
combinations on CD-ROM.  What could be really exciting is
the service market.  Retail internet services, fax machine
upgrades to X.400/TCP-IP (dial internet numbers instead of
telephone numbers), forwarding, X11 configuration files
and services, and so on.  The primary thrust would be
add-ons to the POSIX system of your choice rather than
which overpriced operating system do you want to
fight with.  The applications ads would have 20 or
30 icons indicating which Systems it supports and
the systems ads would have 20 or 30 icons indicating
which applications standards were supported.

	Rex Ballard


