of Alpha at all).

In article <PREECE.92Mar27143807@neptune.urbana.mcd.mot.com>, preece@urbana.mcd.mot.com (Scott E. Preece) writes:
|> On the other hand, one could say that crashme is the moral equivalent of
|> jabbing a screwdriver into the innards of your Walkman and then
|> complaining that it wasn't built sturdily enough.

It's not equivalent, since software doesn't wear out or break in the same
sense that hardware does.  User-level programs should not be able to crash
an OS, period.

|> I don't want to appear to be defending unrobustness -- certainly we're
|> working on hardening against the problems that crashme turns up and a
|> multiuser system does have to protect itself against malicious behavior.

|> On the other hand, system vendors have limited resources and have to
|> choose whether to spend them on things like defending against deliberate
|> attempts to tickle the hardware into failing or on things like adding
|> functionality and improving performance.  Since *most* sites are peopled
|> by cooperative users who *don't* spend their time trying to crash the
|> system, this is not as simple a choice as it might appear.

Unfortunately, university sites on the Internet have become exactly this
kind of environment; the number of crackers and vandals has been increasing
exponentially.  If crashme can accidentally (by generating and executing
random code) crash a system, or, worse, get the system to execute code
in kernel mode from an unprivileged account, crackers will exploit this.

Problems that crashme detects are usually in the exception handlers in the
OS, and are very rarely in the chip.  They do happen: a CERT advisory reported
that it was possible to obtain root under some version of SunOS by integer
division by zero!  (Presumably there was some problem with the trap handler
that it was possible to exploit).

I strongly recommend that test engineers test the effect of executing random
code on all CPU/OS combinations.


-- 
Joe Buck	jbuck@ohm.berkeley.edu


