In article <1992Mar19.150237.7309@crpmks.uucp>, billb@crpmks.uucp (Bill Bochnik (Info Systems)) writes:
> In article <203558@unix.cis.pitt.edu> sillett@edc3jr.gsph.pitt.edu (Robert L. Sillett) writes:
>>
>>My department currently has a VAX 6000-310 which is heavily loaded.  I
>>was looking through DEC Direct yesterday and saw that the MicroVAX
>>3100e is rated at 3.5 VUP's (versus 3.8 for the 6000-310).
>>
>>For the price, the 3100e looks like a great little VAX.  However what
>>is the weak link in this MicroVAX?  Is it really almost as fast as a
>>6000-310?  Or does the SCSI bus slow it down?
>>
> 
> This has been a great subject of debate here.  We had people saying that they 
> had these great workstations that had the same compute power as our bigger 
> vax's.  This mat be true, but they are strictly single user systems (although 
> you can have more that I person on at a time).  

I disagree somewhat with the above.  It all depends on what the workload
on the 6310 is.  If it's CPU bound, the 3100 would likely come close.  However,
if it's I/O load, the 3100 doesn't come close.

I was recently involved with a company that had a small, simple application
that they wanted to run in each of several offices around the country.  They
chose the 3100 as a platform to replace various NCR mini systems.  The largest
of these sites had a nominal user load of 40 to 50 users.  The 3100 performed
fine;  most of the users were doing data lookup with occasional data entry.

This site also had a medium size Vaxcluster made up of ageing 8600 and 8530
CPU's.  Some of the less technical management concluded since a 3100 could
support 40 to 50 users, they could move their large M&D financial packages
and databases to 3100's.  Some benchmark runs proved otherwise...the systems
just could not handle the I/O load of heavy RMS ISAM activity.  In short, it
it depends on what the workload is.

Size of the physical box is becoming less important.  I recall talking to
some of the Digits at Decus about the 6000 series packaging.  When asked
about all the airspace in the cab, one of the engineers replied that they
had several smaller prototypes, including systems based on the 8200 style
cabs, and something similar to a BA123, but Marketing felt that customers
wouldn't believe that a real computer could be in such a small enclosure.

> Putting many users on a high 
> end workstation dogs the machine (the bus cannot handle the load).  The 
> microVax is similar.  

I'd hardly call the Microvax 3100 a workstation machine.  It's a nice
processor for a moderate size group.

> You can put many people on it, but you will not get the 
> performance of the larger machines.  The bus is slower, and clustering is 
> slower as you would probably use ethernet rather than CI as Digital does not 
> market a Q bus Ci adapter.  

There are people who swear by a 3rd party board, which Digital now remarkets...
I admit, I have reservations about the Qbus bandwidth limitations;  however, if
I had a "cheap" MVII or III for development (read, cheap licenses) and a
large production cluster, such a board would be a perfect way for the little
Vaxen to share the disk farm.


Anyway, if you want to post more details about your workload, I'll be 
happy to discuss further, here or in mail.

Finally, one other option you might look at is the annual Digital
Outlet sale.  (the end of the year firesale).  They are offering 
a 6310 to 6320 upgrade for $33,000 and a 6320 to 6420 for $44,000.
That's around an 80 percent off list.

  Charles Smith
  cts@dragon.com


