Path: sparky!uunet!snorkelwacker.mit.edu!world!aps
From: aps@world.std.com (Armando P. Stettner)
Subject: Re: Question for net.views column in UNIX Today!
Message-ID: <BLH35t.Bpt@world.std.com>
Summary: MSDOS vs. UNIX
Sender: Armando Stettner  aps@world.std.com  aps@berkeley.edu
Organization: The World Public Access UNIX, Brookline, MA
References: <1992Mar17.044404.22677@ucsu.Colorado.EDU> <1992Mar20.002532.64733@frodo.cc.flinders.edu.au> <sperry.920320161259@bailey.cpac.washington.edu>
Date: Sat, 21 Mar 1992 16:58:41 GMT

People seem to be missing the weapons and tactics; they have some
component of the battle field, namely UNIX vs MS-DOS or Windows or NT.
The real weapons is all those applications.  The majority of the world,
which is probably not represented in this news group, buys these smaller
systems for reasons that don't necessarily include flexibility and power
of the user or programmer interfaces or even necessarily hetergeneous
interconnection.  Most of the people buy these smaller systems for the
applications they can run on them.

They can get these applications at 7-11, some corner computer store,
some book stores, or from the growing number of places which answer 24
hours a day and can overnight the packages such as PC/Connection or
MAC/Connection, etc.

NT is sort of the next step in the Windows evolution.  The industry
landscape in this (evolving) price performance market should be real
interesting in the future.  If UNIX is to survive, let alone, succeed at
being a market leader outside of us who can navigate it, it may have to
support an MS-DOS/Windows applications programming interface.

	aps.

P.S.  The neat thing abou being an independent is not having to disclaim
anything.


