tihor@acf3.NYU.EDU (Stephen Tihor) writes:
>With all the new generation systems getting guarenteed ship by dates is
>important.   It used to be you could rely on DEC not to announce
>something until they were ramped up but now even they are playing  the
>game and program announcing things months before they can ship and thus
>mothes before the last engineering bugs are worked out.

	The "ship date game" is getting really ridiculous - there are a
lot of vendors taking the approach of "we'll gladly take your money
today for a workstation tomorrow" and DEC's starting to get into that
game too. :(  As usual, it's not something that is likely to stop, since
vendors will be encouraged to make "strategic announcements of a strong
corporate intention to pre-announce a program to announce a chip that
will be the core of a system we plan to ship sometime before we get
senile" as long as customers take the numbers at face value. It's
the old "lock 'em in" game - nail down the business and then ship as
late as you can. It's disgusting, it's sleazy, and - it works.

	As long as customers keep buying stuff based on pre-announcement,
the pre-announcement times will keep slipping out farther and farther
as vendors rush not to develop new machines, but merely to announce
them, in hope of nailing down market share. Non-disclosures will be
jacked into the realm of science fiction. ("by the year 2030 we will
be ready to program announce a 350BIPS machine")

	I've gotten so sick of it that I completely refuse to believe
in (or suggest that anyone buy) anything that I haven't seen, smelled,
shaken, or kicked - and you can't kick a "program announcement". (Yes
I have seen and shaken Alpha machines and I believe in them)

	It'd be nice if some of the giants among the customer base
would start to flex their muscles in favor of a little sanity. One
good de-book of a huge award for failure to ship on time *might*
be enough to put a stop to the current trend. Too bad you can't
write penalty clauses into the contract so that the vendor has to
cut the price by some percentage for "lost computing resources"
for any delay in ship. Makes sense, no? Charge 'em as if they were
a timesharing user for every potential MIP wasted. ;) Hell, just
depreciate the machines...

	I spoke out of turn with my remark about the SGI machines. I
don't know their shipping schedule. All I know is that *I* have been
told it's somewhat aggressive. The individuals who told me that could
have been wrong - only time will tell. I definitely was out of line
and in the future will keep my mouth shut on the topic of anyone's
ship dates but our own.

	I personally favor a Dominos Pizza-type policy: If the machine
is late, the next one is free (or you get free pepperoni on it, or
something like that).

mjr.


