In article <22262.29c20c31@amherst.edu>, jwmanly@amherst.edu writes:
|>How does the SCSI port and the Ethernet adapter on the various workstation
|>models compare to each other? For example, the SCSI bus on the VAXstation 3100
|>Models 30 and 38 are known to be very poor performers.  What about the  newer
|>VAXstation 3100 Model 76, and even better, what about the new VAXstation 4000
|>units?
|>

The problem with a "single number" is even more pronounced when you start 
talking about I/O performance than when you talk about CPU performance.  
For instance, the I/O speed is dependent on many things, including OS overhead
(which version of the OS are you running) and block size.  Smaller block sizes
make smaller I/O performance since the overhead is a larger percentage.  The
disk attached to the other end also makes a great difference.  For instance
the RZ23 (ASynchronous) and RZ58 (Synchronous with large caches) will perform
vastly different.


However, you'll find that the VAXstation 4000 family has significantly faster
I/O than the prior generation VAXstations.  This is due to two enhancements
that we've made:

	1:	The SCSI has been "upgraded" to a Synchronous SCSI  using
		the 53C94 chip instead of the older Asynchronous SCSI on the
		3100 family.  In theoretical bandwidth,  that means that the
		SCSI bandwidth has gone from 1.5MB/Sec to 5MB/Sec

	2:	The 4000 family implements "Direct DMA" to the users buffer
		instead of the block copies that the 3100 used to do.  The 3100 
		used DMA to go into a system buffer, but the driver then had to 
		do a block-for-block copy to/from the user buffers before the
		transaction was finished.  The 4000 skips this overhead and
		does the DMA right into user space.  Much faster



I believe that Digital Review  did a test of the I/O of the 4000 in early 1992, 
that may help you with your table, but be very careful when comparing that you
compare apples  to apples



