In article <1992Mar19.003842.11239@midway.uchicago.edu>, newark@befvax.uchicago.edu (Tom Newark) writes:
|>
|>When they released the VaxStation 4000 dec said it would offer a 24-bit
|>graphics option for said maching.  Does anyone know the status of this option.
|>It is a really sad state of affairs that dec has been dragging it's feet so 
|>long in the graphics area on their vms machines.  Let's hope they don't jerk
|>around with their alpha products in the same way... 

The announcement of the new graphics options is tomorrow (March 24th), they 
will start shipping by the end of the week.  The new options, the SPXg and 
SPXgt will provide 8 (SPXg) and 24 (SPXgt) plane support with  hardware
accelerated 3D.  
|>
|>Also, why did dec only put one turbo channel slot on the 4000?  Is there any 
|>way to get a turbo channel extender? .....  I mean what ever happened to
|>workstations with 8 Qbus slots?

A workstation with 8 Qbus slots is 1) slow, and 2) costly.  The 4000 has a
single TURBOchannel slot for I/O expansion.  Realize that the graphics options
and most of the other I/O don't take a TURBOchannel slot.  (the DECstation for
instance requires TURBOchannel for both graphics and I/O)

The TURBOchannel extender is not supported because the hardware doesn't support
"logical" (or addressable) extension, just physical extension.  What that means
is that you have the option of putting a single board that takes more than one
slot in the box, however you can't put multiple options in the TCE.  Given
that, and that the only multiple-slot options we know of are graphics (which
are on the internal graphics connector anyway) there is little reason to 
support the TCE.

|>This seems to me to be a very poor design
|>move (the only thing dumber was giving the vs 3100 no bus and an extremely poor
|>scsi controller).  Why have they been dumping so heavily on their vms products?
|>What do they have against expandability?

The vast majority of the workstation market needs no expansion, which is 
where the VS3100 and VS4000 machines were targetted.  However.  Our surveys
indicated that the lack of a bus on the 3100 was  a problem for our customers
and that a single slot  bus (we hadn't invented TURBOchannel yet) would satisfy
the vast majority of those who had I/O expansion requirements.  Given that, and
the lack of space in the enclosure, we did a single slot TURBOchannel.

We have nothing against expandability.  The issue is providing the right amount
of expansion so that you satisfy the majority of the customers without costing
them too much for something they don't need.  (A pedestal Q-bus system with 
a VS4000-like system would be  in the $15K entry price.  Would you rather have
that, or a VS4000-60 with a $6K entry price?)



