jona@iscp.Bellcore.COM (Jon Alperin) writes:

>In article <1992Mar22.082347.12023@aplcen.apl.jhu.edu>, jarober@aplcen.apl.jhu.edu (DE Robertson james an 410-740-9172) writes:

>|> ALL. To the average user, DOS is more 'open' because he/she is not limited
>|> to a specific vendor. One can mix any number of different vendor's PC 
>|> clones and be assured that WordPerfect will run on all of them. Contrast that

>|> Jim Robertson

> NOT!

> Try taking a look at all the PC based magazines (Byte, PC week, Info Week,
>etc.). Every one of their help column tends to focus on "special" workarounds
>that must be implemented for a specific package, for a specific architecture,
>and at a specific release. 

	Note that I said the average user, not the overworked network
administrator. I have been an admin for a SUN network, and have heard
my wife recount horror stories on DOS LAN's. I would much rather
administer the typical UNIX based LAN than deal with a DOS LAN. However,
the average user is interested in the running of a few applications - 
word processors, spreadsheets, email. Those of us in the programming world
often forget that most people never compile a single line of code. Most
people have little or no interest in it - they want to be able to load their
application and forget it. 

	My point was this: Say you have the sort of Unix environment that
we have at my site (which happens to be a federal agency). There are
SUN workstations, DEC workstations, HP's, DOS boxes, Xenix boxes, SCO
Unix, etc.... In order to standardize on a single word processor for all
secretarial staff, multiple vendor versions (not all at the same release 
level either) must be bought. The workstations are expensive as well, at
least when compared to a DOS box. If all you really need is word
processing, why spend 7K when $1500 will get you a box perfectly capable of 
running literally thousands of applications ? 

	Second point: DOS stinks as an OS (if you even want to go so far as
to call it an OS). But.... At the end of the day, no harm is done by
turning off the power. In a non-networked environment, DOS does not 
require a seperate admin person, whereas UNIX will require a knowledgeable 
person to spend some time with it. The only company I see making any 
strides at all in this direction is SUN with it's SOLARIS effort. The ability
to run DOS applications will be critical to it's success - like it or not, 
there are just too many DOS users and too many DOS apps to ignore.

> When you start adding memory managers, TSR's, etc. to DOS, everything
>suffers. I wouldn't want to have to produce PC software in this day and
>age, since there are simply to many products that you need to test and 
>interoperate with. On the network side you have Novell, Lan Manager,
>plain ol' TCP/IP, Banyan Vines, and a few more. On the application
>side there are at least a dozen memory managers that make DOS do things
>it was never designed to do. Even OS/2 has similar problems. U*IX just 
>has de facto standards (sockets, streams) that makes networking a snap,
>and similar admin facilities (not exactly the same, but fairly close
>to one another), and more. One app may work on a variety of platforms,
>but who uses just one app?

	BTW, since when were sockets/streams standards ? In the unix world, it
is a BIG jump from a system V sdstem to a BSD based system. Heck, to joe
user it's a big jump from a SUN running OpenWindows to a DEC Ultrix system
running MOTIF - even though they are BOTH BSD based systems. The user 
interface is what the user sees first, and it is what drives him/her to 
decide whether a system is 'hard' or 'easy'. Should Microsoft get Windows
NT out the door as a working 32 bit system they will have one tremendous 
advantage over the rest of the market - while OSF and SUN/UL argue
over who has the better interface, Microsoft will be claimning to have the
'standard' interface that user's already know. I don't really want to argue
over which is 'better' - because in this domain, that is totally irrelevant.
Marketing is what matters. As a previous poster said, don't ignore a man with
far more resources and a bigger installed base than you.



