>It's nice that clock speeds and CPU performance are being kicked up,
>but I would sure like to see the same degree of improvement in storage
>systems.  Having these souped-up CPUs with current storage technology
>is like driving your 'Vette in first gear...

	Generally this is a problem that's attacked on three fronts:
1) work harder (make faster disk drives) - work continues here
2) work smarter (newer better ways of doing I/O) - log based file systems
	and other filesystem enhancements can provide an order of magnitude
	more throughput than current filesystems
3) work different (try something else) - stuff like NVRAM caching and
	other tricks - RAIDs and systems to take advantage of parallelism
	are crucial here as well and all play together

	Obviously, drives are getting faster all the time. New versions
of UNIXes will be showing up with LFS' and whatnot (like BSD4.4) and
that will help write speed. Ever growing caches will help read speed.
NVRAM will speed synchronous writes.

	Look for all of the above from just about every vendor, including
DEC in years to come. I/O is a HOT topic and everyone's looking to
distinguish themselves.

