                                3P SIG Activities 

BACKGROUND INFORMATION




DECUS SPRING 1992 SEMINARS AND SYMPOSIUM PROGRAMS TO FEATURE INFORMATIONAL
FORUMS FOR THIRD-PARTY PROVIDERS TO THE DIGITAL MARKETPLACE

   Third-party hardware, software, and service providers doing business in
the Digital marketplace will meet with their peers, learn skills, and be
provided with information designed to aid the development of their business
strategies, during the DECUS Spring 1992 Seminars and Symposium programs, May
2 - 8, in Atlanta, Georgia.

   Through the efforts of the Chapter's Third-Party Providers Special
Interest Group (SIG) and Digital Equipment Corporation - developers, system
integrators, and consultants focusing on both business and technical issues
will use the symposium to discuss a range of topics including:


* Business issues, including Digital partnership arrangements, licensing
  practices, proprietary information disclosures, etc.

* Support issues, including Digital to third-party providers, third-party
  providers to their end users, and end users receiving support from multiple
  parties.

* Product and technology directions, including hardware/software
  architectures and standards.

* System integration issues, including packaging, portability,
  interconnectability, interoperability, upgrading, reliability, and
  performance issues.

* Internationalization issues, including product design, import/export
  requirements, etc.

   A total of 37 third-party-related sessions are scheduled to be presented
during the symposium week featuring speakers from both Digital and
third-party organizations doing business in the Digital marketplace.
Symposium attendees will have the opportunity to participate in a variety of
clinics and panel discussions, as well as an Open Steering Committee meeting,
and a session focusing on "Future Directions".

    The Working Groups of the Third-Party Providers SIG, including:
Independent Software Vendors, Independent Consultants, Resellers, and System
Integrators - will each hold general discussion, and "Interact with Digital"
meetings during the symposium.

   The group is also sponsoring a full day seminar on Sunday, May 3, focusing
on starting and operating a successful consultant practice.

Technical Update

   Digital's Open Systems for 1992: Tools and Platforms for Applications
Developers and System Integrators - a full-day, Technical Update sponsored by
the Third-Party Providers SIG and Digital, will be presented on Friday, May
8.

   Digital will use the Technical Update to present technological and product
implementation information expected to be of critical importance to
third-party providers. Digital will share specific information on their
future open systems, most notably the extensive Network Application Support
(NAS) "Middleware" products for DEC and non-DEC systems, the 64-Bit RISC
"Alpha" family of systems, the OSF/1 and SCO UNIX operating systems, and the
extensions to VMS.

   A primary emphasis of the Technical Update will be to provide developers
with advance information on migrating their applications to Digital's 64-Bit
RISC systems.

Topics to be covered include:

* VAX VMS Hardware and Operating System Software Futures
* 64-Bit RISC Hardware and O.S. Environments: VMS and UNIX
* MIPS RISC Systems and O.S. Environments: OSF/1 and SCO UNIX
* Intel Systems and PC LAN Products
* Workstations and PC Systems
* Digital's "Middleware" NAS Products
* Database Products
* CASE Software Development Tools for DEC and non-DEC Systems

   Due to the proprietary nature of discussion, attendance for the Technical
Update is limited to representatives from firms approved by Digital Equipment
Corporation to receive this information. DECUS will review all Technical
Update registration requests with Digital for approval.

