              DECUS U.S. CHAPTER SPRING SEMINARS AND SYMPOSIUM
                    SATURDAY, MAY 2 - FRIDAY, MAY 8, 1992


Shrewsbury, MA - February 28, 1992 - The U.S. Chapter of the Digital
Equipment Computer Users Society, DECUS, will hold its Spring Seminars and
Symposium programs, May 2 - 8, 1992 at the Georgia World Congress Center,
Atlanta Marriott Marquis, and the Atlanta Hilton and Towers, Atlanta,
Georgia. The semiannual event, considered by many to be the most
comprehensive and cost effective forum on working with products from the
Digital and Digital-related arenas, provides attendees with the tools
necessary to succeed in their computing environments.

   The events begin on Saturday, May 2, and Sunday, May 3, with the DECUS
Seminars program. DECUS Seminars are high quality, in-depth, training
sessions, presented in both full- and, two-day formats - each focusing on a
specific technical topic.

   The Spring 1992 Symposium, which runs from May 4 to May 8, will consist of
over 1,100 hours of sessions which offer attendees the chance to participate
in a wide variety of tutorials, clinics, and workshops. Like the DECUS
Seminars program, Symposium session speakers are drawn from the day-to-day
users' community, as well as the educational and engineering communities of
Digital Equipment Corporation and other leading professional organizations.
Symposium sessions address virtually every aspect of computing with Digital
and Digital-related products. Streams of interest include: artificial
intelligence; data management; graphic applications; languages and tools;
networks; security; ULTRIX and VAX systems; and much more.

   The combined events will allow participants to meet with an estimated
4,700 of their peers - to share experiences, solutions, and to build a
network of valuable industry-wide contacts that can be referred to for years
to come.

   DECUS Symposium attendees will also hear the latest developments and be
aided in their preparations for the release of Digital's new 64-bit RISC
architecture - code named "Alpha". The Chapter's VAX to Alpha Systems
Transition (VAST) Initiative and the VAX Systems Special Interest Group, in
cooperation with Digital, will present over 30 separate VAST sessions during
the symposium week. Theses sessions will provide the most up-to-date
information on: Alpha chip technology; porting, programming, and migration
issues; Alpha workstations, and much more.

   The group will also sponsor a full-day technical presentation featuring
members of Digital's Alpha Project Team, on Saturday, May 2, entitled -
"Digital's 64-Bit RISC Architecture: Application Coexistence and Migration".

   Advance information on Alpha will also be the primary focus of the Spring
1992 Technical Update for Third-Party Providers to the Digital Marketplace,
on Friday, May 8. Presented by the Chapter's Third-Party Providers Special
Interest Group, in conjunction with Digital, this full-day non-disclosure
program, open to selected applications developers, integrators, and
consultants, will provide a rare look at future Digital products and plans.

   The Spring Symposium will feature a keynote address given by David Stone,
vice president, Software Products Group, Digital Equipment Corporation, on
Monday, May 4, at 8:30 a.m. in the Georgia World Congress Center. Mr. Stone
will address concurrent engineering and implications in a world of
distributed LANs.

   During the symposium week, Digital representatives will staff a hands-on
exhibit hall providing attendees the opportunity to experiment with a variety
of both the Corporation's established and cutting-edge products and
technologies.

   DECUS Symposia also provide face-to-face contact with a number of Digital
product developers and engineers, allowing attendees to get answers to
specific questions, discuss solutions, drive the direction of future product
development, and establish valuable technical contacts within Digital.

   A complete program, including registration information, is available from
the DECUS Office, SHR1-4/D33, 333 South Street, Shrewsbury, MA 01545-4195 or
by calling (508) 841-3341.

   DECUS, the Digital Equipment Computer Users Society, is currently in its
31st year of helping to promote the free and open exchange of
information-processing-related information among the users of Digital
Equipment Corporation products. There are over 48,000 members in the U.S.
Chapter. In addition to the semiannual seminars and symposium programs, DECUS
members solve their organization's computing problems, and stay abreast of
industry issues and emerging technologies, with the aid of the Society's
electronic conferencing system, regional conference and seminars programs,
quarterly technical newsletter, and public domain software library.

