In article <1992Mar19.151545.18127@mixcom.com>, xxwwxx@mixcom.com (Tim and Tim Company) writes:
   > 
   > 
   > There would appear to be a small bug with the VT220
   > terminals I use.  This bug was not really evident until
   > I started poking around with an MNP modem.
   > 
   > It seems that when you have the VT220 set on "Smooth Scrolling",
   > it won't send Xon/Xoff when the buffer is full.  This happens
   > with 2400 baud modems as well, but to a lesser degree.  When the
   > MNP modem is communicating any higher than 2400 baud, and smooth
   > scrolling is enabled, characters, and sometimes whole blocks of
   > text are missed.  I've even tried using it with Modem Control,
   > but to no avail.  I haven't tested this theory with a data
   > line from the computer yet, but should that make a difference?

I don't think xon/xoff will work in those circumstances. The modem will just
pass the ^S and ^Q to the other end, which will have to handle the
flow control. Since MNP modems have internal buffering there may already
be enough data in the modem buffer to overflow the terminal buffer, even
if the remote end stops sending.

I think you will have to use CTS/RTS flow-control to handle that situation
which requires a suitable 7-wire cable between the modem and VT220, and
appropriate setup for the VT220. Can a VT220 handle that? Not all
DEC terminals can, I think VT100s cannot, VT131s can. I can't remember about
VT220.

Steve

