I suspect this might sound a trifle heretical on comp.unix.ultrix but
basically I'm getting sick of the hassles with ultrix on a humble
VAXstation II and wonder, what with all the alternate u**ces popping
up, whether it's time to ditch ultrix for a better u**x.
The main hassles are kernel bloat and lmf (even with edu discounts
it's getting hard to justify the cost of the annual s/ware licences).
I'm hoping there's a stable u**x that runs lean and mean on a stock standard
VAXstation, has one-time costs scaled for an antique VAX, and has no
time bombs masquerading as an lmf.  Even just binary 4.3BSD would do ---
don't want or need whatever's fashionable this week.  An X11R4 that
drives the mono display (qvss) thrown-in would be nice.  TCP-IP
stuff that works and NFS is a must.  LAT would be cool but I can learn to
live without it...

Has anyone gone with a non-ultrix u**x on a VAX II or its kin and therfore
have any recommendations (or the opposite)?  If there's enough interest
I'll gladly summarise.  Seems only a matter of time before interest in
os-es for old VAXen will follow as per pdp/lsi-11s...

PS: I've got nothing against ultrix per se.  On a DECstation or even
a gruntier VAX it's great, and I've happily used and recommended it since
version 1.0 days.  It's just that I'm no longer sure it's the way to go
on this much-loved and trusty old iron.

PPSS: I've though about going back to pre-lmf and pre-bloated versions
such as ultrix 3.0 or even (gasp) 2.2 but each was badly broken in some
critical way while DEC tinkered away at getting it right :-)
and ultrix 1.0 didn't even have NFS.

Alex.

