From: mmccarri@physics.ucla.edu (Mike McCarrick)
Subject: Re: XOFF problem with HP printer on DS5000/200
Message-ID: <1992Mar16.220159.23928@physics.ucla.edu>
Organization: UCLA Department of Physics
References: <1992Mar13.191235.29572@physics.ucla.edu>
Date: Mon, 16 Mar 92 22:01:59 GMT


In <1992Mar13.191235.29572@physics.ucla.edu> I wrote:

>I am trying to attach an HP Laserjet III (w/ Postscript) to my
>DS5000/200 running Ultrix 4.0.  The printcap entry is included at the
>end for your information.

>The problem seems to be that the DECstation doesn't respond quickly 
>enough th XOFF's from the printer.  That is, if I eavesdrop on the 
>serial line, I can see that the printer sends an XOFF, the DS continues
>sending, the printer then sends a bunch more XOFF's and then complains
>about and I/O Config error (buffer overflowing).  The DS does eventually
>stop sending data, but only after overflowing the printers input buffer.

>Has anyone else seen or solved this problem?  Is it a problem with the 
>HPLJ sendin XOFF too late (when the buffer is too close to full), i.e.
>is there another printer that would work correctly?

I received a couple of requests to post any solution, so here it is:
The needed patch came from Klaus Harbo <harbo@euromath.dk>, which he
in turn received from John Voss <j.voss@ucq.edu.au>.  The patch is 
applied to /usr/sys/machine/common/conf.c and goes like this:

> 
>     *** /usr/sys/machine/common/conf.c.orig	Tue Feb 12 10:46:23 1991
>     --- /usr/sys/machine/common/conf.c	Tue Feb 12 10:47:53 1991
>     ***************
>     *** 1072,1077 ****
>     --- 1072,1078 ----
> 
>       #ifdef mips
>       int	cnopen(),cnclose(),cnread(),cnwrite(),cnioctl(),cnselect();
>     + int	cnstop();
>       #endif mips
> 
>       #ifdef vax
>     ***************
>     *** 1896,1902 ****
>       #endif vax
>       #ifdef mips
>	  /* d_ttys will be filled in by cninit */
>     !	cnioctl,	nulldev,	nulldev,	0, 
>	  cnselect,	nodev,		0,	0},
>       #endif mips
>	  {dzopen, 	dzclose,	dzread, 	dzwrite,	/*1*/
>     --- 1897,1903 ----
>       #endif vax
>       #ifdef mips
>	  /* d_ttys will be filled in by cninit */
>     !	cnioctl,	cnstop,		nulldev,	0, 
>	  cnselect,	nodev,		0,	0},
>       #endif mips
>	  {dzopen, 	dzclose,	dzread, 	dzwrite,	/*1*/
> 

After rebuilding the kernel, the printer now works perfectly! Thanks Klaus!

Regards,
Dr. Mike McCarrick
Plasma Physics Lab, UCLA


