	If it's a real UNIX, presumably there *will* be some license
cost hidden away there, since AT&T doesn't give UNIX away. Digital
has to keep track of ULTRIX user licenses used, even *internally*,
since we pay AT&T a royalty for each and every happy UNIX user.

	This is why they call UNIX an OPEN system. ;)

	The only way to avoid licensing with UNIX is to use a UNIX
like operating system - you could try to get MACH or Sprite or XINU
or something like that for your VAXstation. The other way is to steal
it, which I don't recommend.

	I assume by annual s/ware licenses you're referring to software
maintenance? This is distinct from just the license - you actually
get updates to the operating system and so forth in return for your
money. If that's a problem, why not just drop your maintenance and
stick with what you're running.

>An X11R4 that
>drives the mono display (qvss) thrown-in would be nice.  TCP-IP
>stuff that works and NFS is a must.  LAT would be cool but I can learn to
>live without it...

	You complain about ULTRIX being bloated and then turn around
and demand X, IP, NFS, and LAT? That's a veritable "microkernel" you
want there. ;)

	If you're an academic, you can try to get Plan9 from Bell Labs,
and port it to the VAX. *THAT* is a small operating system without
lmf and even better, without NFS or X...

