In article <28953@skye.dcs.ed.ac.uk>, dhd@dcs.ed.ac.uk (Damon Hart-Davis) writes:
> >9)  Will it be a good workstation engine, and why or why not; if so, when?
>  
>         Should be.  Late 1992.

The Alpha sounds very impressive -- now. But clock rate isn't anywhere
near everything and I am wondering how actual *systems* will perform
relative to the competition at the time when they become available. My
guess is that it will still look good, but not spectacularly so.
Performance seems to double every 18 months to 2 years. How long is it
since we had 60 specmark workstations? It seems like we *should*
expect 120 specmark workstations soon. A short delay in time to market
can do a lot of damage.

Which leads me to wonder. They reportedly have Unix running on the
alpha chip now and yet they appear to be waiting until they have VMS
and OSF/1 running before releasing systems. Granted there is a
difference between getting Unix to boot and having a robust release,
but I can't help wondering whether the impact of the Alpha
architecture might be lessened by marketing decisions to only release
it when VMS is ready.

