dall@hfrd.dsto.oz.au (Ian Dall) writes:

>Performance seems to double every 18 months to 2 years. How long is it
>since we had 60 specmark workstations? It seems like we *should*
>expect 120 specmark workstations soon.

	That assumes that the chips can actually run at the speeds
required to make a 120 SPECmark workstation. That's not necessarily
the case - just because a current PA-RISC or SPARC chip can run
N Mhz today doesn't mean that it can necessarily handle a higher
speed later on. It gets into all kind of bizzarre effects that I
don't fully understand, but basically as the clock speed gets higher
and higher, weird things start happening. It's my understanding
that Alpha chips have a lot of headroom to grow ("grow" being a
misnomer - really what is meant is "shrink" since as the clock
speed cranks, the chip has to get smaller due to little issues
like the speed of light)

>but I can't help wondering whether the impact of the Alpha
>architecture might be lessened by marketing decisions to only release
>it when VMS is ready.

	We'd be pretty dumb to delay Alpha until VMS was ready if
we had a commercial ready OSF/1. Remember that operating systems are
never "ready" - there's an endless continuum of "more and more ready"
so, yes, both VMS and OSF/1 run on Alpha today - they'll run better
in more time.

	My understanding is that there are a lot of compiler issues.
Every time the compiler guys think of some neat new optimization for
the Alpha, suddenly the operating system may grab a few more AIMs. I
used to be pretty frustrated myself that we weren't heaving Alpha
machines over the fence as fast as we could make them, but it's
possible the current strategy is a good one. We want the thing to come
out running fast and rock solid.

