I would call your local DEC sales rep and ask him the following questions:

(1) How much will he charge you for additional 6000-300 processors 
   (just processor cards) refurbed (if available) and new?

(2) How much will he sell you an 6000-300 to 6000-400 processor upgrade?
    (The -400 processor is rated at 7 VUPS. You might find it would be
    worthwhile (fiscally) to go from a 6000-310 to a 6000-420.)

If he can't help I'd check and see what you can get in the used market ala
additional processors or processor upgrades.

Lets not forget that your 6000-310 can go to a 6000-340 which is
4 processors. (I think actually it can goto 6 processors or -360.)

Now ask yourself how much have you invested in in your storage subsystems?

Do you really just need more computes?  Or more storage and computes? Or
more memory, storage, and computes?

The 6000 class machines cost a little more initially than other systems *but*
increasing their performance should be less expensive than most other options.

>
>For the price, the 3100e looks like a great little VAX.  However what
>is the weak link in this MicroVAX?  Is it really almost as fast as a
>6000-310?  Or does the SCSI bus slow it down?
>

Depends on whether you application is IO bound (hence the load) or CPU bound.
The SCSI bandwidth is fairly reasonable unless you want *lots* of simultaneous
transfers.

>We would like to purchase a 3100e and run System 1032 on it,
>supporting 10-15 users (which we do on our 6000-310).

are you going to run it standalone?  if so, you're gonna have to buy all new
(SCSI) disks and use those.  If you're going to cluster it you will be limited
to the ethernet for IO.  Ie. ~1MB/s - ethernet load.

The 3100's have some things going for them:

You don't need a computer room.  They're cheap.  The disks can be purchased
from a third-party (I'm not saying I'd recommend it).  On the other side you
can't add a heck of a lot of memory to them compared to a 6000-310.  If your
users are both memory and CPU intensive- and you have less memory on the
3100 than you have in the 6000 class machine you might find that your 3100
is performing substantially worse than you'ed expect.

>
>Thanks
>
>--- Bob
>

As a general rule of thumb you need to consider the following when replacing
or substituting systems:

Initial Investment: Capital Costs, like Air Conditioning Units, computer rooms
                    Big Compute Iron.

then your recurring costs:

Depreciation
Field Service and Maintenance.

