I suppose I should comment on this, since it couldn't be much further
from the truth. In July of
1979, I joine the RSX engineering group, taking over the development of
mP support from Kim
Kinnear.

DEC built three different mP hardware platforms in the 11/74 program.
The first was an 11/70
modified to have multi-port memory, using existing bus switches
(DT03,06, and 07), and an
interprocessor interrupt mechanism which was a variation on the DIP11
CSS option. The ASRB
instruction was modified to provide an interlock mechanism. (ASRB on a
lock with a 0/1 value
provides an atomic test and set). This system was referred to as 11/70mp.

This system worked, but memory contention was pretty horrendous. The
resulting changes
resulted in the KB11-CM processor, aka the 11/74. This system had a
different cache controller,
a different UNIBUS map, and shared memory (It has 7 different modules
and [I think] 22 wire
adds to the 11/70 backplane.) 

We also did, as was eluded to previously, a KB11-E processor, with
integrated CIS instructions.
the KB11-CM won, and was pressed as the product offering.

RSX ran pretty much fine on the 11/74. In fact, one of the first things
I did was to move the
RSX development timesharing/build activities onto our Quad 11/70mP. The
project was
cancelled in January of 1980, not for technical reasons (and no, it
wasn't because it ran
faster than the 780, either. We never actually compared them.) I didn't
like the decision, but
I was forced to agree with the facts and logic behind it.

When RSX engineering moved to the Spit Brook Engineering facility in
September of 1980, we
put up a new Quad processor 11/74. When the project was cancelled, we
had a significant
inventory built and ready to ship (100+ units), so coming by processors
was easy. We used
that system for RSX-11M-PLUS production until 1990, when the cost of
monthly service
forced us to retire it. During those ten years, the only system downtime
we experienced
(other than crashes) was when we voluntarily gave the entire system to
field service for
problem solving. I suppose I should mention that the system had user
configurable
connections so that it could be configured around failed components. So,
we had near
100% availability for ten years.

When we retired the system, the CSC in Colorado Springs took it and
brought it up. The
attached RMD and CON display from this morning should show that all four
CPUs, although
not very busy, are still functioning.

