In article <1992Mar13.040608.39921@camb.com>, bruce@camb.com (Barton F. Bruce) writes:
> The KXT11 mentioned above used the T11 chip and WAS NOT I/D. There was
> a later card called the KXJ11 that was a J11 based card. Stuffed into
> a Q bus microvax, one or more KXJ11s could run the Co-Processor RSX product
> DEC offered under VMS.

Unless there was a major revision to the KXT11 card, all of the KXT11's
I saw were not capable of running virtual memory in any meaningful way.
I don't remember offhand if they had complete implementation of the
mapping registers, but they were definitely not split I/D;   and since
they only had about 32KB of memory or thereabouts the issue of mapping
18- or 22-bit memory management schemes was pretty much moot.  Really
strange device - lots of oddball ports and DMA controllers quite unlike
anything else in the PDP-11 line.  I'm afraid I no longer have either
a KXT or its documentation, although somewhere on some of those old 8"
floppies I probably have a fair amount of software I once wrote for them
(8-).  They made pretty nice controllers for various devices that you
could plug into the Q-bus (unlike other PDP-11 processors they were
designed to be run as a Q-bus slave rather than a Q-bus master).

The KXJ-11 was a much nicer product, but by that time we no longer had
any interest in the KXT-style controller market.

Bruce C. Wright


