In article <491@ais.com> arthur@ais.com writes:
[...]
>RSX-11D was actually the direct parent of IAS.  Originally, RSX-11D was
>a strict priority realtime system.  As a field test site, I and Herb Nichols
>of DEC finally got a timeslicer to work and got the bugs out of swapping
>various types of images and shared libraries.  This is all during the
>1972-1974 period, before RSX-11M was written.  M was the scaled down version
>that could run on machines without memory mapping (<56kb total).  Much of
>the insides of M appear to have been borrowed directly from 11S by Dave Cutler
>while the Files-11 F11ACP and similar items came from 11D.  11D cam 
>prebuilt and was sysgened in one step; 11M came in source mode and built
>forever with TKB.  IAS was a timesharing system built upon 11D; M+ was 
>somewhat similar, but based upon 11M.
[...]

A time-slicer for RSX-11D wasn't too hard a thing to do. Just a privileged
task that scans the ATL every few ticks or so and when priority changes,
picks the node of lower priority and reinserts by priority. That round robins
at a given priority.  It's no big deal then to expand it to change the
priority based on heurisitics.  Or to ignore tasks in the ATL that are in
other than RUN state, e.g., those that are waiting on I/O. This worked pretty
well on RSX-11D V2. I sold one to DEC CSS for enough money to pay for the
delivery charges for my second kid :-)

My memory of the history of RSX is that:
The first to show up was RSX-11C, a system that had only QIO disk support with
no file system. This was followed a bit later by RSX-11B, a system with limited
file support. With both of these, program development was done under DOS-11.
The first version of RSX-11D to show up (1C?) also required DOS for generation.
The first viable version of RSX-11D was V2, followed by V4, V6, V6.1, and V6.2.
I think that IAS showed up between 6.1 and 6.2. Odd numbered versions were internal
or field test.

RSX-11A was a neat, tidy little system that showed up at about the same time as
RSX-11M, but required program development to be done under DOS-11 or RT-11. I liked
RTS/8 better, though :-)
