In article <1992Mar12.033502.12153@massey.ac.nz>, news@massey.ac.nz 
(USENET News System) writes:
> Greetings,
> 
> Does anyone out there use a VR290 color monitor?
> 
> i desperatley need help with how to setup the function keys. the docs i
> have are not helpful. Getting access to the function keys is crucial so i
> can talk to unix machines, where among other things, i require an esc key
> (for emacs) and a backwards apostrophe. curretly, the VR290 does not allow
> me this basic necessity.

You already have a backwards apostrophe -- it's on the same key as the tilde,
to the left of the 1 ! key.  

Some programs will recognize F11 as Escape.  Unfortunately, some won't.  

> BTW, mine is attached to a GPX Workstation, VMS5.4.

Let's explain a few things first... the "VR290" is just the monitor. You're
running under one of:  VWS (VAX Workstation Software), DECwindows, or
DECwindows/Motif.  What you are asking for is support for definable function
keys in the window system's terminal emulator. 

DECterm gives you this ability -- see the _DECterm Text Programming Manual_.  
You will have to write a small amount of code (or at least DCL) to send the
correct escape sequences to your "terminal" before connecting to a Unix 
system.

BUT -- for the keys you mention -- you have a much easier workaround, at least
under DECwindows and Motif.  Don't know about VWS. 

Go to the "Customize" or "Options" menu from the DECterm menu bar, and select
"keyboard".  There's a button there that lets you cause the ` ~ key to become
an ESCape key.  Well, now you have ESCape conveniently, but you've lost your
left apos. and tilde.  So, there's another button that lets you make the angle
brackets key (to the left of Z) act like the ` ~ key used to, sending left
apos. if unshifted, and tilde if shifted.  But now you've lost angle
brackets... but a third button causes the , and . keys, when shifted, to send <
and >, just like they used to on the VT100 keyboard.  In fact, the whole 
result looks a lot like the VT100 keyboard, except for the odd position of the
` ~ key.  

That should do it.  If you're running VWS, I hope there's something similar.  

