In article <1992Mar12.184718.6050@tin.monsanto.com>, lesnyd@monsanto.com (Larry Snyder) writes:
> I remember writing devices drivers for both RSX11D/IAS and RSX11M. The 
> driver for IAS was called when an event occurred, such as I/O requested, 
> I/O rundown, etc. The lineprinter driver had a little problem in that when 
> the printer went offline, it blocked, waiting for it to come back online. 
> If a process attempted I/O rundown it would stop at the printer driver 
> (all drivers get called for I/O rundown) until the printer came back 
> online. This meant that I would hang up on a control-C until the printer 
> was put back online!

This was, unfortunately, a fairly common problem with a number of the
drivers for RSX-11D/IAS, both DEC and third-party drivers.  The drivers
for various serial devices (other than the terminal driver) were the
worst, the disk and tape drivers were a lot better.  I think a lot of
the problem was that quite a few of the people who wrote the drivers
never really got the hang of it, with the result that there was a lot
of really bad code out there.  If it wasn't a really common driver then
it didn't get fixed (I still remember rewriting the paper tape drivers
to fix this problem, I think we must have been about the only site that
used them ... but we had some laboratory equipment that insisted on
punching a tape and we had no good way to just hook it up to a terminal
line ...).  There were various problems with some incarnations of the
LP handler, I think that it finally got fixed in later versions.  

Actually if I recall, the OS didn't call *ALL* drivers on an I/O
rundown, but only went through the I/O database queueing the I/O
rundown packet to each device until the task's I/O pending count went
to 0.  By ordering the I/O database so that the "bad" drivers were
last you could avoid about 98% of the problem without code changes.

> I felt the RSX11M drivers were more elegant, with I/O request nodes, event 
> flags and all that spiffy stuff. 

Well, yes and no, there were some things that you just couldn't do very
well in RSX-11M drivers, and a lot of the ugliness that was found in
some of the -11D/IAS drivers wasn't the fault of the OS driver model but
rather the result of incompetent coding (including by DEC).  But doing
an -11D driver right took a fair amount of code (by the standards of
the day, only a K or 2 was significant code ... you wouldn't think
twice about that now), and -11M drivers could be done properly in much
smaller space.  But they didn't scale well to large systems ... too
many problems like lack of address space etc.  

> But both were fun and easy to write!

Yeah!! 

Bruce C. Wright


