|>the 32 bit emulation mode (read: backwards compatability for older 32
|>bit VAX/VMS binaries) will consume cpu cycles off of the 32 bit Alpha
|>VMS implementation.  Ergo the emulated software will run slower.  I have
|>read figures 20 to 40% off the top (of Alpha) for emulated software. 
|>Even with 40% off the top, it will *still* be faster than on any VAX
|>processor. 
|>
|>The native 64 bit (when it eventually comes out in some future
|>undesignated date) implementation will be an improvement to the 32 bit
|>implementation, however.  The improvement will primarily be from the
|>increased address space (an additional 4GB).
|>


I think you are confusing the 32 bit VMS environment with the
VAX image compatibility features.

The 32 bit implementation of VMS is native Alpha code.  Running
at top Alpha speeds. (The 21064 isn't in a 32 bit mode.)  The VMS
operating system is just ingoring the top 32 bits.   I think
that you can still write a program that uses 64 bit integers in 
this environment.  (But will that program work back on the VAX?)

Digitals current VMS emphesis is on compatibility with VAX.

The VAX image compatibility features.   Alpha has a translator that 
translates VAX instructions to Alpha instructions.   The translated
image is executed.  Any code that can't be translated gets interpretted
at run time.   I believe that the 20 to 40% numbers are the 
performance comparisons of translated-from-VAX code versus native compiled
code.   Last I heard was that translation is automatic (transparent
to the user).   So, when you get your Alpha machine, all your programs
will work.  They will all run significantly faster.  Additional performance
will be obtained when your programs are updated to the native Alpha versions.


Paul Wisner  (not an Alpha engineer, not an official Alpha spokes person)

