My understanding was that RSX-11D (which I never used, but was told was 
rather much of a dog) was written by some "ordinary" OS group in DEC.  
Then the (in)famous D N Cutler came along and wrote 11M in his spare 
time (or some such story).  11S was then a stripped-down 11M, and IAS 
(which disappeared just as thoroughly as 11D) was a beefed up 11M.

We got our first VAX before 11M+ arrived here, so I don't know much 
about it.

>                                              RSX had some strange ideas
> about terminal i/o that I think have mostly not been carried over to VMS,
> and some very strange ideas about storage of data in files, which
> unfortunately have been carried over to VMS (the FILES-11 file system,
> level 1):  version numbers in file names, file types in the operating
> system such that each application has to have code to read and write any
> file type it might be asked to operate upon, different i/o operations for
> files and devices.

I don't think RSX was terribly wrong about the _idea of_ the device
driver/RMS split; it enables you to build stuff into your shared code 
that is otherwise stuck into every application.  There's a valid 
argument that says that RSX's implementation (and VMS's thereafter) of 
RMS was cumbersome, however.

I've said enough here: there's potential for a terribly exciting 
argument about the merits or otherwise of structured files here...  ;-)
