>>mjr@hussar.dco.dec.com (Marcus J. OpenRanum) writes:
>>> randy@rls.UUCP (Randall L. Smith) writes:
>>>> Not exactly.  There will be a 32 bit software emulation mode that Digital
>>>> acknowledges will be slow, but at least it will be there.
>>>
>>> VMS will run in 32 bits and it will run faster than it does on a VAX. 
>> 
>>Sure it will.  It's running on a processor screaming along at 200Mhz. 
>>There are currently no processors running at this high of a clock speed. 
>> 
>>What I meant (and thought I said) was, the software implementation of
>>the 32 bit emulation mode (read: backwards compatability for older 32
>>bit VAX/VMS binaries) will consume cpu cycles off of the 32 bit Alpha
>>VMS implementation.  

As stated in another note, there is no 32 bit emulation mode.  VMS runs
using 32 bit addresses (so the top longword of each address is either 
-1 or 0).  

You cannot take a VAX VMS binary and execute it on Alpha VMS.  Rather, there
is a binary translator that takes a VAX VMS binary (shareable or main) and
translates it into an Alpha binary.  The resulting Alpha image contains
Alpha code and runs on Alpha VMS.  There is support in an Alpha VMS RTL that
enables the translated image to run as if it were running on a VAX.  

>>Ergo the emulated software will run slower.  I have
>>read figures 20 to 40% off the top (of Alpha) for emulated software. 
>>Even with 40% off the top, it will *still* be faster than on any VAX
>>processor. 

The degree of "VAX-ness" required directly affects the performance.  So
performance will be good if you require no VAX-like behavior, and a bit 
worse if you require things like support for unsupported data types 
(like H floating point or exact exception reporting).

