11/45 Vs 11/55          (From my PDP11 04/34/45/55 Handbook 1976/77):

  "      The 11/45 is a powerful 16-bit computer....up to 128k words
        addressable memory space.....  will operate with solid state and
        core memories...choices of 300 or 495 nanosecond memory, an
        advanced Floating point processor and sophisticated memory
        management.  Included in the basic 11/45 were:  16k words mem,
        Choice of bipolar and core mem, ...mounting space for FPU and
        MMU. 

Features were:

        Mem expansion to 256k bytes...optional FP11-c FPP, Fast Secondary
        bus between proc and solid state mem in parallel with
        unibus....etc.  "


"The 11/55 ....designed to accelerate FORTRAN compiled tasks....

Features include:

        300ns dual ported bipolar memory, High speed FPP  with 46
        instructions,;  Internal microinstruction cycle time of 150ns;
        Instr execution in 300ns;  Instruction pipelining via next instr
        prefetch; FPP calculation independant of main CPU; Dual bus for
        memory;...  up to 256kb memory (up to 64kb bipolar alone)...three
        cpu op modes (kernel, super, user)...hardware memory mgmt...two
        sets of 8 general purpose registers....etc. "


                         ----------------------

        As a technician in a previous life who worked on
        11/20/05/40/70/34/04 etc, but not 11/45/55. the '55 is all that an
        11/70 was with the exception of the Bipolar memory and a few
        other tweaks.


Mike, whose opinions are his own.


