OK. Let's get to the facts. I was around back then and ran RSX-11D, M, S, and
IAS on a variety of systems. The history posted here is close, but not quite
right.

The Resource Sharing eXecutive came out of work done at the University of
Washington. I don't remember the name of the head of the project, but it
produced what became known as RSX-11A (or, at the time, just RSX). It was not a
multiuser system and looked little like any of the "commercial" RSX OSs. It
look a bit like the early flavor of D. It allowed multiple tasks to share the
processor, switched on the basis of "events", not time. So it was Resource
Sharing instead of Time Sharing.

In RSX-11D V4, the concept of multiple independent users appeared, but problems
with the multi-terming TT driver kept from actually working until V4U came out.

RSX-11D continued through V6B, I think.

In the mean time, the legendary D. Cutler got tired of fighting the mass of
code in the OS when doing development and, according to rumors, wrote a slimmed
down kernel which he dubbed RSX-11M for internal use by developers. Supposedly
he did it over the Christmas holiday. Its largest virtue was the use of memory
mapping in the system to allow it to run with far less memory. 28K was usually
adequate. It also replaced the RSX-11D device handlers which were separate
tasks with the executive linked drivers.

Meanwhile, back in jolly England a bunch of folks designed a real time sharing
system for "large" PDP-11s. It was designed to be an OS for scientific and
engineering use, as opposed to the educational background of RSTS. It was
called the Interactive Applications System (IAS). As RSX-11M caught on with
those looking for small OSs, IAS and RSX-11D started competing for the same
market with similar products. DEC realized maintaining both was silly and
merged the two as IAS. It did both time sharing and resource sharing, even both
at the same time for the foolhardy. But its biggest contribution  was the
command syntax that grew and eventually became DCL. Before the IAS command
processor, to copy a file you used the command:
PIP DK0:[120,10]APPL.FOR = DK1:[23,14]OLDAPPL.FOR (in some cases the "<" was
used in place of the "=".) 

IAS was really a very good OS, but it required, at the very least, a PDP-11/45
and about 64KB of core.  An 11/70 with 2 MB was even better. And that was an
awfully big system in those days. Worse, development was in the UK and, as a
result, DEC US looked upon it as a poor relative. Most US salesmen had never
even heard of it! Buying it was almost impossible and it would have died an
early death if one of DEC's largest clients bought into it. Pressure from this
client with the support of us small fry kept IAS alive for many years.

As stated, RSX-11S was a single tasking version of "M" that could be down
loaded to an application specific PDP-11.

My memory may be off just a little bit on some of this, but I ran every one of
the PDP-11 systems, as well as DOS-11, RT-11 V1.0 on DECtape (who remembers
DECtape?), and PTOS-11 (the Paper Tape Operating System). I remember links on
the DECtape system that ran for over 16 hours.

