The 'invalid media format' message means, basically, that VMS is seeing an
unformatted piece of media.  Easy enough, there.  I've seen this message
appear occasionally on RD53s if they've been up & running for several years--
it seems to be the case that these drives "forget" their formatting on random
occasions and need to be reformatted using the diags.  But if this drive was
just formatted then perhaps the drive or RQDX2 have problems, or there's a
mismatch between RD53 and RQDX2 PROM level that takes awhile to show up. 

I had a friend call recently to ask "what would happen during a VMS 5.4 to 5.5
upgrade that could cause a VAXserver 3900 to stop acting as a boot node?  It
seems that his machine would no longer accept satellites booting from it.  He
could SET HOST to it, though, so I had him do that and check NCP, CLUSTER_
CONFIG, and some other stuff.  Then he suddenly lost his SET HOST connection, 
and I had him call DEC F.S. to check the DELQA.  Sure enough, the damn thing
had blown sometime during the upgrade...made it seem like a VMS issue instead
of hardware failure!  Murphy again...

