To begin with, I don't know what plans there are for whether translation is
static or dynamic; so I am blissfully ignorant and can blather on without
giving any corporate secrets away....

I will point out that in OSF/1 the dynamic loader can dynamically load
dynamic loaders; it would be easy to recognise a MIPS binary, and at run
time do the binary translation into memory.  In fact, Larry Allen, who implemented
the OSF/1 dynamic loader once implemented a loader which would allow you
to directly run an LZ compressed executable; it invoked the decompressor
on the fly and decompressed the executable into memory and then branched to
the result.  Clearly this would be (at least at current processor vs. disk
speeds) a bad idea for frequently used executables, but the day may come;
I also have no information on how fast the binary translator runs.

My only point is either static or dynamic translation are feasible.
