In article <1911@augustana.ab.ca>, bjorndahl@augustana.ab.ca 
(Sterling G. Bjorndahl) writes:
> We have a 2400 baud V.42bis modem on a MicroVax II RS232 serial port.  The
> modem wants to use CTS as hardware flow control, to tell the uVax when its 
> buffer is full.  But the uVax just keeps on sending data anyway, even when
> CTS is raised.  Thus people dialing in miss huge chunks out of their e-mail
> or editor screen or whatever.

You didn't say what operating system you're running, but under VMS, if the port
is configured for modem control (SET TERMINAL /MODEM), CTS flow control works
just fine.  RTS does not (the VAX doesn't do anything with RTS except keep it
'true' most of the time), but the VAX will stop sending data (within a few
characters, even at 19.2 Kbps) to the port if CTS goes 'false', and will resume
sending when CTS goes 'true'.  I've watched this happen on a line analyzer many
times.  It works not only with DHVs but with all DH-series muxes except the
CXA16 (but including the CXY08), and with all other VAX serial ports that
support "full modem control".  

This isn't documented, but it's been working this way since at least mid-V4.  

