> In article <BKxt5F.DzB@world.std.com>, geoff@world.std.com (Geoff Collyer) writes:
>>[lots I don't want to comment on]
>>> RSX is a multitasking OS. Author of RSX developed VMS and is now working
>>>         with another party, involved in a major project for them.
>> 
>> As you noted in your original message, RSX is actually a family of
>> similar operating systems:  RSX-11S at the low end, RSX-11M for the
>> mid-range and RSX-11D (aka IAS) for 11/70s.
> 
> My understanding was that RSX-11D (which I never used, but was told was 
> rather much of a dog) was written by some "ordinary" OS group in DEC.  
> Then the (in)famous D N Cutler came along and wrote 11M in his spare 
> time (or some such story).  11S was then a stripped-down 11M, and IAS 
> (which disappeared just as thoroughly as 11D) was a beefed up 11M.
> 
> We got our first VAX before 11M+ arrived here, so I don't know much 
> about it.
> 
RSX-11D was actually the direct parent of IAS.  Originally, RSX-11D was
a strict priority realtime system.  As a field test site, I and Herb Nichols
of DEC finally got a timeslicer to work and got the bugs out of swapping
various types of images and shared libraries.  This is all during the
1972-1974 period, before RSX-11M was written.  M was the scaled down version
that could run on machines without memory mapping (<56kb total).  Much of
the insides of M appear to have been borrowed directly from 11S by Dave Cutler
while the Files-11 F11ACP and similar items came from 11D.  11D cam 
prebuilt and was sysgened in one step; 11M came in source mode and built
forever with TKB.  IAS was a timesharing system built upon 11D; M+ was 
somewhat similar, but based upon 11M.

There was strong competition between the 11D and 11M groups, with the 11M 
group acquiring the upper hand mostly because of the number of licenses
(much fewer $ each, on much smaller systems).  I had the privilege of using
and supporting every flavor mentioned above -- 11S (and its DOS-11 partner),
11D, 11M, 11M+, IAS, and POS (the M+ variant for the Professional 300 series).


