>>> As you noted in your original message, RSX is actually a family of
>>> similar operating systems:  RSX-11S at the low end, RSX-11M for the
>>> mid-range and RSX-11D (aka IAS) for 11/70s.
>> 
>> My understanding was that RSX-11D (which I never used, but was told was 
>> rather much of a dog) was written by some "ordinary" OS group in DEC.  
>> Then the (in)famous D N Cutler came along and wrote 11M in his spare 
>> time (or some such story).  11S was then a stripped-down 11M, and IAS 
>> (which disappeared just as thoroughly as 11D) was a beefed up 11M.
>> 
>> We got our first VAX before 11M+ arrived here, so I don't know much 
>> about it.
>> 
> RSX-11D was actually the direct parent of IAS.

Arthur pretty much has it the way it actually happened, but he left
out a fair amount.  The original granddaddy of the RSX family was a
funny little memory-only system called RSX-11A.  It was sort of like
a very early version of RSX-11S, but only the broad outline of the
architecture was the same -- even the system call formats were changed.
This evolved through RSX-11B and RSX-11C before RSX-11D was developed
that actually supported substantially what we thought of as the basic
RSX-11 features, including disk-based file structures, real-time,
shared libraries, and multi-user.  Somewhere around RSX-11D V6A (later
renamed V6.0) the timeslicer that Arthur refers to was written by Nichols
and others - before that RSX-11D pretty much was a dog, but by V6.2 the
timeslicer was pretty solid (ca 1977).  It was around that time period
that -11M first appeared commercially, and it too was pretty bad until
the bugs were shaken out -- my introduction to -11M was finding an
infinite loop in its secondary bootstrap!!

RSX-11M and RSX-11S were developed by Dave Cutler by reworking RSX-11A
to use the RSX-11D system call formats, and importing things like the
Files-11 ACP (F11ACP) into it (before this, RSX-11A had disk drivers
for use by real-time programs, but no file system [!] - it was usually
used in conjunction with DOS-11, booting between the systems depending
on whether you needed the real-time or the file system ... in those
days [early 70's], RSX pretty much meant laboratory instrumentation
and process control rather than timesharing).  RSX-11M was disk-based,
whereas RSX-11S, like RSX-11A, was intended as a "stand-alone real-time"
system and was intended to run on a minimal PDP-11 without a file system.

IAS was basically RSX-11D with an added layer of "time-sharing services"
and a DCL CLI (as opposed to the old MCR CLI whose ancestors dated back
to RSX-11A).  The timesharing services on RSX-11D have their equivalents
on VMS with the concept of process and job associations, subprocesses,
device protection, and so forth.  (All versions of RSX allowed you to
spawn new tasks but they were essentially "detached processes" to use
the VMS terminology;  IAS, like VMS, had a more tree-structured hierarchy
for timesharing tasks).

Meanwhile -11M was improved considerably, and finally some of the same
timesharing features of IAS were imported into -11M+, including DCL.

The structure of the VMS internal executive interfaces (like device
drivers) is most like that of the RSX-11M Executive interfaces, possibly
because the same person wrote much of both of them :-).  But at a user
level, the feel of VMS is not much unlike the feel of an enhanced IAS (or
to some extent M+) and not much like the feel of RSX-11M.

> 11D came prebuilt and was sysgened in one step; 11M came in source mode
> and built forever with TKB.

At one of the Decus Symposia from back in the late 70's there was a
button I wanted to get but unfortunately it "sold out" quickly --
a white butten with the words

		TKB FOREVER

in block letters.  Priceless.  (TKB == Task Builder == Linker).

Bruce C. Wright


