This is the most interest I've seen on INFO-PDP11 in a long time.  I could
hardly miss the volume of mail, but one of my colleagues insisted on sending me
one of the articles on RSX-11D and IAS.

As has been pointed out, RSX-11D is the common ancestor of the IAS, RSX-11S,
RSX-11M, RSX-11M/PLUS, and TSX operating systems.  It was introduced circa 1972
and was designated as the operating system for the larger, UNIBUS PDP11 plat-
forms.

RSX was an acronym for Resource Sharing Executive.  RSX-11D was classified as a
real-time, multi-tasking, and multi-user system.  By today's standards it was a
very small operating system.  It's principal attributes were a small Executive
that provided basic services.  Device handlers were not part of the Executive. 
They were written as independent, privileged application programs.  The files
system, FILES-11, was implemented in a similar manner; however a portion of the
file system was incorporated in each application program.

      Mach, OSF/1(?), and Windows/NT operating systems follow the RSX-11D
      implementation scheme--a relatively small Executive whose primary
      function is to manage resources and route requests to independent
      application programs that provide the requested service.

One of the advantages of this operating system architecture is that allows
complex device handlers to be implemented with little effort.  The biggest
struggle in developing a device driver for RSX-11M/PLUS or any other operating
system where device drivers are part of the Executive is trying to match the
orignal development team's pre-conceived notions of I/O operations with the
requirements of the device.

The first task of any operating system development effort is to implement the
basic disk and terminal services.  Somehow the I/O mechanisms needed to support
those devices never seem to match the I/O mechanisms required for a 32 channel
communications multiplexer that can support different protocols on each chan-
nel.

Another basic feature of RSX-11D was sharable code and data regions.  It was
inherently required to allow the device handlers access to the Executive's data
structures and the I/O page.  This feature was also available to application
programs.  Pure code sections were inherently shared between all copies of a
program running at the same time.

It was also a multi-user system.  There was no need to define partitions and
multiply install a program in several partitions as required by early versions
of RSX-11M.

IAS (Interactive Application System) was introduced circa 1978.  It was devel-
oped in Reading, England as a time-sharing system.  RSX-11D provided the basic
operating environment for IAS.  The IAS Timesharing Executive was implemented
as an RSX-11D device handler.

To limit access to the underlying real-time system, an English-like command
language interpreter was developed for IAS called the Program Development Sys-
tem (PDS).  This was ported to RSX-11M/PLUS and renamed the Digital Command
Language (DCL) and became an integral part of the Verbose Message System (VMS).

IAS had been intended to be the operating system for the PDP11/74 which was
intended to be a time-sharing system; however, there was one basic problem. 
The operating system was being maintained in England and the hardware was being
developed in the US.  Enter RSX-11M/PLUS derived from the integrated RSX-11M
operating system.

While the IAS/RSX-11D software architecture was flexible in dealing with vari-
ous devices, this flexibility made it extremely difficult to provide a fail-
over mechanism when one of the PDP11/74 cpus failed.  Aside from that, there
was an existing RSX-11M operating system for a quad PDP11/45 system that I had
worked on (1975-1977).  It needed a lot of work to re-configure it for general
computing use but it was a lot easier to adapt than to try and design a mecha-
nism for IAS/RSX-11D to notify each handler to ignore that UNIBUS fault and
restart the I/O operation because its now running from a different processor.

RSX-11D and IAS continued to exist as separate products until about 1983 when
IAS 3.0 was released.  With this release IAS could be configured during system
generation as a real-time RSX-11D system, a time-sharing IAS system, or a hy-
brid multi-user system.  The latter used a simple time-slicing mechanism in-
stead of the more complex kilo-core-tick time quantum of the time-sharing sys-
tem.

This was also the time when Digital began openly talking about discontinuing
support for IAS with intent of moving customers to VAX systems.  This worked
with many of their commercial customers; however, their two largest customers
were AT&T and DoD who had built large communications networks based around the
RSX-11D operating system architecture.  Aside from cost, neither RSX-11M/M PLUS
nor VMS had a suitable environment for supporting the existing communications
equipment.

Since the release of IAS 3.2 in 1985, an enhanced version of IAS called DoDIIS
IAS has been used by DoD.  DoDIIS IAS which I've been responsible for develop-
ing and maintaining will be retired next month.  Replacing it will be IAS 3.5
which will incorporate full support for third-party mass storage devices that
were part of DoDIIS IAS and DoDIIS Communications Software for IAS (DCSI) 1.0,
a layered product, which provides support for the communications interfaces.

I probably should note that IAS now runs on all Q22 and UNIBUS PDP11 platforms
including the PDP11/93 and PDP11/94.  It supports all T/MSCP devices.  And,
will run on the PRO 350 and PRO 380 that you might still have hanging around in
the back of the lab.  You're still stuck with DECnet Phase III but do have
TCP/IP networking capabilities.  The latter is provided in DCSI and, also, can
be purchased from Process Software.

DEC and GTE will be providing support and enhancements to both products through
the year 2000.  They may not be widely known; but, definitely, are not dead as
has been suggested.

Merton Campbell Crockett
Manager, DoDIIS Communications Software for IAS
GTE Government Systems Corporation
Intelligence and Imagery Processing Organization.



