Did I miss it, or did no one mention RSX-11M+ which could very
definitely be gen'd to use I/D space separately and to use supervisor
mode resident librarys. They vectored the res libs and in a later
version vectored the exec, and then sysgen time dropped to almost
nothing as lots of priv things needing to be mapped to the exec didn't
need to be rebuilt. 

F77 and other compilers could easily build separated I and D sections.
Without overlays, tasks could be twice as large. 

Another M+ trick, which I am pretty sure they never took out even when
no hardware to run it on still existed, was the multiprocessor support.
This was put in to run the 11/74s which you could have 4 of running
together! 

At the high end of the spectrum, the new HOPPED-UP 11/70s that have had
all old memory boxes simply removed, and had BOTH sets of Setasi mods
installed are incredibly faster than ANY other 11. Setasi's products are
sold by DEC and other channels, and the design stuff is all by the same
Dr. Jones that worked for DEC in the 11/70/74 era, and I that I think
did his thesis on the 11/70 cache (probably BEFORE it was 11/70 cache
:-) ). 

These souped up 11s are running many critical systems TODAY, and more
are still being built and bought! Setasi has licked the CPU speed
problems for these old work horses, and now is attacking the I/O issues in
an interesting way. 

The 11/70, unlike all other 11s (except the 11/74 variant, of course), 
had MassBus into the CPU, bypassing the slow Unibus. The old MassBuss 
peripherals are now the MAJOR maint expense and capacity limitation of 
these machines. 

Setasi is taking large arrays of the very latest and extremely FASt SCSI
optical drives and running their signals through an engine that can do
really any other interface desired (load new microcode, and plug in
suitable adapter cable to desired BUS), but they are making it for
MASSBUS usage! 

Folks doing Police/Fire/911 dispatch or international funds transfer
need and can well afford the brute speed and head-crash proof
reliability these combinations bring. 

So 11s live on, and there are models yet to come (but maybe not from
Dec). 

I wonder if Alpha VMS will still have RSX emulation...
I hope they put I/D mode back in if they do.


