	This is the advertisement for new VMS software product -
fast file viewer LOOK.

	We VAX users know the problems damn well: I want to see
first the 100th line of a file & then the 3rd; or else, I want
to see not only some template in file, but also it's context.
And in addition, the total file size is 1,000 Kbytes (it contains
50,000 lines of absolutely uncomprehendable Assembler text), & therefore
I could have a good sleeping waiting for a text editor to load it.

	Now the solution of these problems exists! Using LOOK software
you will never spend time waiting near the empty screen - LOOK software
will show you the first page (20 lines) of your file just immediately
after you invoke it. And surely you can read the file in any order,
and found template (marked by bold characters) will appear just in
the middle of the screen - within a page-size context.

	To be brief: here is the list of advantages of LOOK software.
- Random order of file reading (with the help of standard cursor keys,
  <PREVSCREEN>/<NEXTSCREEN> keys, hotkeys 'R' - Screen Right & 'L' -
  Screen Left, 'H' - home, 'E' - end; besides, using hotkey 'W' you can 
  toggle screen width - 80/132 columns mode (of course if your terminal
  support 132-column regime); using hotkey 'G' you can enter line number or
  file fraction (in percents) to go to; surely a keybar is always present
  in the last line of the screen);
- Awful fastness (the time of first page appearing simply doesn't depend on
  file size);
- Two modes of file contents representation: text & hexadecimal (in
  format of DUMP utility; you can throw off the sequential DUMP at all
  if you have LOOK software); the initial mode is chosen by LOOK software
  automatically (TXT for text files & HEX for binary); & you can toggle
  them anytime (pressing hotkey 'X' or '/');
- Processing of list of files (sequential): you can specify file list
  as simple enumerating (example:  LOOK TEST.C TEST.EXE ) or using wildcards
  (examples:  LOOK *.MAC  or  LOOK T%%%.*  or even  LOOK [...]*.*  -
  sequential view of all files in all your subdirectories);
- Search for given template (case-insensitive, forward); surely in HEX mode
  you can seek for a byte (or byte sequence) by its hexadecimal code; note
  that given templates (up to 20) are buffered (like commands in DCL) &
  kept during entire work session (i. e., file list processing); so, you
  can really never use SEARCH utility having LOOK software - instead of,
  e. g.,  SEARCH *.MAC "CLRL" , you enter  LOOK *.MAC , press hotkey 'F',
  enter a template CLRL, after first finding press hotkey 'N' for next
  search, and when nothing will found in the first file, press hotkey 'X'
  to go to next one; then simply pressing 'N' you will seek for the template
  in this next file; in any time you can press hotkey 'Q' and quit LOOK
  software at all;
- Spawn facility (rather useful in VMS);
- Possibility to invoke text editor (by default LSE) with positioning to
  line just being watched; you can install any other text editor;
- Self-tutorial HELP (hotkey 'P');
- LOOK software never knows such barrier to file access
  as 'FILE CURRENTLY LOCKED BY ANOTHER USER'; i. e., you can see file just
  when some other process reads it or writes to it (to follow current
  changes of file contents use hotkey <CTRL/R> to reread file once again).

  When LOOK software executes some prolonged command (i. e. search in a large
file) the diagnostics appears with the approximal file fraction just
processed (in percents); you can press hotkey 'X' to cancel it.

    *** You will have real pleasure with VMS only using LOOK software! ***

					Best wishes, Serge Kovalyov.



P.S. Considering that in Russia every transmitted kilobyte is paid, and we
are too poor to create a file server to spread out our programs (as it is
usual for free software), - we ask you to shelter LOOK software on your
server.


