Sender: news@shodha.enet.dec.com
Organization: Digital Equipment Corporation
Lines: 28

There is no such thing as "32 bit emulation" or "32 bit mode" on ALPHA.
There is only one "mode", and its 64-bits.  It is a 64-bit machine.
Period.

All talk about "binary translators" vis a vi "32-bits" is for existing
VAX or MIPS executables and has nothing to do with native ALPHA images.

What people may be thinking about, VMS-wise, is that the VMS system
service interface uses 32-bits for passing addresses.  The interfaces
can't be changed without breaking existing applications that code to
those interfaces.  That makes for some limitations on practical virtual
address size for applications using the existing interfaces.  In other
words, if you had an address somewhere over 4GB there would be no way for
VMS to do things like I/O to and from it.  The application would indeed
be using 64 bit addressing, but would never have a virtual address space
that exceed 4GB!
