John R. Levine:
> PDP-1: 18 bit, based on MIT designs
> PDP-4: 18 bit, not particularly compatible with -1

As I recall, DEC produced PDPs 1 through 16, excluding only 13.  I forget
what the 2 and 3 were.

> PDP-11: all new 16 bit, wildly successful
> PDP-12: improved LINC-8 with single integrated CPU, really cool machine

The story I've heard from people who should know is that the 13 was not
skipped out of superstition, but that numbers were not reused once
assigned and the 13 was the design Edson DeCastro and others were working
on.  They wanted to use new technology; DEC (predictably) declined, and
DeCastro left to form Data General.  The design supposedly became the DG
Nova.

> PDP-14: strange little process control box
> PDP-16: stranger set of do it yourself modules

The PDP-15 was an 18-bit machine, descended from the PDP-9, as I recall.
I believe it was sold as a graphics engine; ours came with a vector
graphic display equipped with a light pen and four or five push buttons.
It played a pretty good game of Life, Space War and TECO.  It looked like
DEC wanted to exploit PDP-11 peripherals; ours came with a PDP-11/05 (I
think) as an I/O controller and the disks at least (and possibly the
DECtapes) were attached to the 11, which ran a little OS called Pyrex or
Pirex (PDP-11 something Real-time EXecutive?).  Our 11 had an RS0?
fixed-head disk.  The 11 and the 15 communicated through shared
memory(!), as I recall.  Quite a trick, given that the 11 was a 16-bit
machine.

> See Bell, Mudge, and McNamara, "Computer Engineering" for true facts.

I'll second that recommendation; I think my copy is at home,
unfortunately.
