In article <1992Mar7.180314.8141@decuac.dec.com>, mjr@hussar.dco.dec.com (Marcus J. OpenRanum) writes:
|> 			I wouldn't look for FTP-able patches on the
|> internet in our lifetimes, though. We sell software maintenance, and the
|> guys whose jobs depend on selling it would get pretty testy if we started
|> to give it away. This is reality.

Summary:

Given FTP archives of patches, those people who now buy softyware support
will continue to do so. Those who can't will have less reason to buy from 
certain other vendors (who already make essential patches available).

Look at it this way: 

There are customers who can't afford buying software support 
and there are others who can't afford not buying it.

A big company making money through the use of their computers 
will always prefer software service. Else they might become too dependent
on their software guru(s). But the sysadmin who installs patches gotten 
from an FTP server accepts a large risk, too. 
In case something goes wrong, there has to be be somebody at fault.
As a sysadmin, I'd prefer not being the one. So, I'll try to convince 
the managers to buy software service and will ease my work, looking 
into the patch database and requesting patches via DEC's software 
service, if necessary. 

This particular institute, where I now work as a sysadmin (and get 
to choose hardware, software) can't afford software service.
We won't pay for it, regardless of the availability of patches from
an FTP server. But we choose vendors not only for their machines 
performance. To get at least critical patches via FTP is a major advantage 
of particular vendors, and it makes a difference in our situation.

Its my responsibilty to keep our machines running and I'd accept 
the risk of installing a wrong patch, while I surely wouldn't want to do 
this working in a commercial environment (where I could get fired for 
an error leading to unavailability of the computers because of my mistake).

IMHO, DEC wouldn't loose any profit by making the patches available.
Those who take the risk of getting them and installing them on 
their machines, probably come from an environment where they 
can't afford software service anyway.
Those who can afford software service, won't want to risk their jobs, 
but will make use of an archive of known patches to request them 
from DEC support, so they don't carry unnecessary responsibility.

Don't you agree, that the effeciency of software support may be increased, 
if thousands of people don't have to call, because of a little error which 
is long known and easily fixed ?

Those who pay for software support, pay for DEC taking the responsibility
for availability of their computers, or because it allows them to operate 
computers without too deep knowledge. 
They'll keep their contracts ...

STefan

PS: We use DEC hardware since our first PDP-8, used a cluster of 
    VAX 11/7x0 and now put most of our workload on DECsystems. 
    We are quite satisfied with them in general and would prefer to stay 
    with them.
    Since (in our opinion) the advent of Alpha will mean the end of the 
    DEC*systems* (with most DECstations soon sold as high end PC's),
    we have to choose a new architecture to work on in the future.
    VMS connectivity won't be of importance anymore, easy access to 
    patches (especially security related) and an archive of known bugs will ...
