Larry Snyder:
>In article <1992Mar9.194033.21005@iecc.cambridge.ma.us> 
>johnl@iecc.cambridge.ma.us (John R. Levine) writes:
>> DEC-20: improved PDP-10
>
>As a former DECsystem-10 jockey, I take strong exception to this statement!

Well as another former DECsystem-10 jockey, I'll agree with John.  I
programmed on TOPS-10 for five years (in Macro-10, Fortran, Simula and
others) and I don't miss it.  TOPS-20 was clearly a superior operating
system (performance arguments aside); it incorporated such radical
notions as processes forking.  Until Unix came along, TOPS-20 and Multics
looked like the best operating systems going.

To pick a favourite nit, a DECsystem-10 was a PDP-10 running TOPS-10 and
a DECsystem-20 was a PDP-10 running TOPS-20 plus appropriate microcode
(it added a "jsys" trap instruction).  A single machine could run either
operating system.

I also haven't seen the KS-10 processor mentioned yet.  As mentioned, the
PDP-6 was essentially the first PDP-10; the first actual PDP-10 was the
KA-10 processor (mine was #14 [in some radix], #13 having gone to Vietnam
with the US army), followed by the KI-10 processor (first one with paging
hardware, supposedly cribbed from the paging box BBN built for the KA-10
to run Tenex, from which TOPS-20 was derived), the KL-10 processor
(allegedly designed by Stanford students and known to them as the
``foonly''), the KS-10 (obscure, probably the brains of the 2020), and
finally the never-released Jupiter, which supposedly could be made to run
almost arbitrarily fast [except for the COBOL instruction set] but the
radiation emissions went up with speed and DEC hadn't decided how much
lead shielding was acceptable when the project was cancelled in favour of
the VAX line (or, in one version of the story, in an executive snit over
COBOL running slowly).

In hindsight, the CPUs probably weren't staggeringly fast, though they
certainly seemed quick late at night, and were further slowed by clumsy
byte accessing hardware, but the I/O subsystem(s) seemed to scream along.
After the 10 line was killed, the first MicroVAX appeared, just as the
VAX line was looking slow and stale, and it was my impression that the
ex-10 people who got reassigned to work on VAXen took one look at the
slow-and-tiny approach taken to VAXen and said `get serious, this is no
way to build real computers; we'll show you how it's done'.  The MicroVAX
II in particular was quite a jump forward.  Then we saw the appearance of
larger VAXen like the 8550 and 8650, which had a more LCGish feel to me,
at least.
-- 
Geoff Collyer		world.std.com!geoff, uunet.uu.net!geoff


