mccrohan@salthl.enet.dec.com:
> RSTS is Resource Sharing/TimeSharing

RSTS was initially just timeshared BASIC; though this later changed.  The
PDP-11/55 was built specifically to run it: it was essentially an 11/45
with 8KB of cache memory in the bottom of memory, as I recall, which was
enough to hold the BASIC interpreter.  With RP disks (wash tubs) on it,
it actually ran lots of users running BASIC pretty quickly.

> RSX is a multitasking OS. Author of RSX developed VMS and is now working
>         with another party, involved in a major project for them.

As you noted in your original message, RSX is actually a family of
similar operating systems:  RSX-11S at the low end, RSX-11M for the
mid-range and RSX-11D (aka IAS) for 11/70s.  RSX had some strange ideas
about terminal i/o that I think have mostly not been carried over to VMS,
and some very strange ideas about storage of data in files, which
unfortunately have been carried over to VMS (the FILES-11 file system,
level 1):  version numbers in file names, file types in the operating
system such that each application has to have code to read and write any
file type it might be asked to operate upon, different i/o operations for
files and devices.

> RT-11 is a Realtime OS with Foreground/Background.... MS-DOS looks a bit
>         like it but with many of the useful bits removed.

A good comparison.  RT-11 wasn't much more than a program loader, some
device drivers and a crude file system (but one vastly simpler and more
pleasant than RSX's, despite the inability to have more than one
directory per disk).  Until late in its life, it didn't even enable the
machine's MMU, if present, so using RT-11 was like using a PC or a Mac:
an exercise in Core Wars.  On the other hand, it was so primitive that
writing a very small pseudo-device driver (to catch power up and down
traps) was sufficient to let the machine pretty much ignore power outages
(given core memory), which was useful in a hospital with bad power and
long patient studies.

> MUMPS was developed by Mass Gen Hosp and widely used for a variety of
>         applications. MUMPS is botha a database and a language.

The language is an extended BASIC, as I recall.
