In article <6128@shodha.enet.dec.com>, berenson@cookie.enet.dec.com (Hal Berenson) writes:
> There is no such thing as "32 bit emulation" or "32 bit mode" on ALPHA.
> There is only one "mode", and its 64-bits.  It is a 64-bit machine.
> Period.
> 
> All talk about "binary translators" vis a vi "32-bits" is for existing
> VAX or MIPS executables and has nothing to do with native ALPHA images.
> 
> What people may be thinking about, VMS-wise, is that the VMS system
> service interface uses 32-bits for passing addresses.  The interfaces
> can't be changed without breaking existing applications that code to
> those interfaces.  That makes for some limitations on practical virtual
> address size for applications using the existing interfaces.  In other
> words, if you had an address somewhere over 4GB there would be no way for
> VMS to do things like I/O to and from it.  The application would indeed
> be using 64 bit addressing, but would never have a virtual address space
> that exceed 4GB!
> .............................................................................

From the last DECUS I got the following pieces of info from DEC:

In its first incarnation, Alpha-VMS will continue to use 32 bit addressing
although it will be running on a 64 bit platform.  The memory management
software is where the translations will be taking place.  This is what virtual
memory is going to look like initially:

------------------------------------
System space     FFFF FFFF FFFF FFFF
     |
     |
     v           FFFF FFFF 8000 0000
------------------------------------
P1 Space         0000 0000 7FFF FFFF
     |
     |
     v           0000 0000 4000 0000
------------------------------------
P0 Space         0000 0000 3FFF FFFF
     |
     |
     v           0000 0000 0000 0000
------------------------------------

A few summary notes:
System space is highest virtual addresses
p0/p1 spaces are the same as VAX/VMS
Bit 31 still determines process or system space
Only 32 bits need to be stored in any data structure
Sign extention of bit 31 sets high 32 bits correctly

Note that there is a lack of s0/s1 space.  It has all been called System space
now.

DEC went on to discuss future movement towards a full 64 bit addressing scheme. 
The plan they have today is to first provide a set of system services that will
allow access to the 64 bit addressable region that will work in the 32 bit
scheme.  The only access to this extended region will be through these special
system services that will allow checkin and out of data segments.

Then the intention is to move towards a full 64 bit implementation of VMS
providing direct access to the 64 bit virtual address region.

Ken



