	Then you should understand how difficult it is to fix software in
a *previous* release!! Things get rapidly insanely complex when you try to
backpatch - the very complexity (IMHO) makes the chance of the patch adding
new bugs grow dramatically. (for example, if a bug is found in the 3.1
ULTRIX kernel in a module that no longer even exists in the 4.0 kernel and
nobody recalls all the reasoning behind why it was done the way it was in 3.1
things might break worse - hardware changes)

	Generally when a critical bug comes in, it's either:
a) already been fixed (so the SPR is mooted)
b) totally tragic, so it is fixed, in which case:
	a.1: the fix depends on the current version (EG: 4.0 sources and
		generating a backpatch to 2.0 would be ludicrous)
	a.2: the fix is more or less version independent and the CSC gets
		a patch if it's something that consistently crashes the
		system
	a.3: the fix is incorporated with generally making something
		work better, and it's no unreasonable to expect the
		customer to use a newer release

	I work for DEC and I myself (as are many of us) am frustrated by
our sometime lack of responsiveness on SPRs. I do think things are getting
somewhat better. The DEC FORTRAN crew are doing a pretty terrific job as
far as I hear in keeping customers happy with quick turnaround on quality
issues. All the favorable remarks they've gleaned have (believe me!) been
loudly re-echoed inside the company by those of us that have been following
it. It's getting better. I wouldn't look for FTP-able patches on the
internet in our lifetimes, though. We sell software maintenance, and the
guys whose jobs depend on selling it would get pretty testy if we started
to give it away. This is reality.

mjr.
