mjr@hussar.dco.dec.com (Marcus J. OpenRanum) writes:
> randy@rls.UUCP (Randall L. Smith) writes:
>> Not exactly.  There will be a 32 bit software emulation mode that Digital
>> acknowledges will be slow, but at least it will be there.
>
> VMS will run in 32 bits and it will run faster than it does on a VAX. 

Sure it will.  It's running on a processor screaming along at 200Mhz. 
There are currently no processors running at this high of a clock speed. 

What I meant (and thought I said) was, the software implementation of
the 32 bit emulation mode (read: backwards compatability for older 32
bit VAX/VMS binaries) will consume cpu cycles off of the 32 bit Alpha
VMS implementation.  Ergo the emulated software will run slower.  I have
read figures 20 to 40% off the top (of Alpha) for emulated software. 
Even with 40% off the top, it will *still* be faster than on any VAX
processor. 

The native 64 bit (when it eventually comes out in some future
undesignated date) implementation will be an improvement to the 32 bit
implementation, however.  The improvement will primarily be from the
increased address space (an additional 4GB).

Cheers!

