Thanks for all the clarifying data on the Alpha, it's much appreciated.

The March 2 issue of Digital News arrived today.  On the front page is
an intersting little table which I've reproduced without permission.

			DEC		HP	Sun		MIPS
Chip			21064		7100	SuperSparc	R4000
Max clock rate		150MHz		100MHz	50MHz		50MHz
Peak MIPS		300		200	150		100
SPECmarks		150		120	70-80		70
Virtual addressing	64bits		64bits	32bits		64bits
Availability		4Q92/1Q93	4Q92	4Q92		Now

Note that the sparc is 32bit; note the R4000 is available now.  So it's
not quite an apples and apples comparison.  A MIPs spokesman was quick
to comment on the availability, saying "It really does a disservice to
pre-announce systems that are a year out."  True for MIPS, irrelevant
in the other three cases.  The big MIPS question is how quick will they
run by end of 92.  All data was supplied by vendors, so I'd assume the
same size grain of salt applies to all the figures.

In my book, it looks like the two critical items are instructions per
cycle and the number of cycles the chip will do.

Also in the articles is a picture of the Alpha on the biggest damned
pin-grid-array chip carrier I've even seen.  Once that sucker goes in
a socket it'll *never* come out without a hydraulic life.  Whew!
