computers to that point.  I can provide a little information about
a few of your points of queries.

1. PDP-10's

There were only four PDP-10 processors brought to market: the KA-10, the
KI-10, the KL-10 (ECL), and the KS-10 (marketed as the 20/20).

2. PDP-11's

PDP-11's were built in two technologies:  TTL and then MOS.  PDP-11's have
a number of architectural options, notably:

	maximum physical address:	16b, 18b, or 22b
	memory management:		required for >16b physical addressing
	#memory management modes:	1, 2, or 3
	separate I and D spaces:	versus combined
	extended instructions:		integer multiply and divide
	floating instruction set:	simple add/sub/multiply/divide
	floating point accelerator:	full floating point instruction set
	I/O:				Qbus, Unibus, or Massbus

Here is a from-memory table of PDP-11 models; I cannot vouch for accuracy!

model	introduced	mips	phys	EIS	flt	modes	I/D	I/O

11/20	1970		.2	16b	n	n	1	n	U
11/05	1972		.15	16b	n	n	1	n	U
11/40	1972		.35	18b	opt	FISopt	2	n	U
11/45	1972		.5	18b	y	FPAopt	3	y	U
11/04	1975		.15	16b	n	n	1	n	U
11/34	1975		.3	18b	y	FPAopt	2	n	U
*LSI-11	1975		.1	16b	opt	FISopt	1	n	Q
11/70	1975		.7	22b	y	FPAopt	3	y	U,M
*F-11	1978		.3	18b/22b	y	FPAopt	2	y	U,Q
11/23	1979		.3	22b	y	FPAopt	2	y	U
11/44	1981		.45	22b	y	FPAopt	3	y	U
*J-11	1983		.6-.7	22b	y	FPA	3	y	U,Q

* = MOS chip

Notes:

The 11/15, /10, /35 were the "OEM" versions of the 11/20, /05, /40,
respectively.

The 11/55 was a variant of the 11/45 with a bipolar RAM main memory for
fastest possible FORTRAN execution.

The LSI-11 was used in the LSI-11 and LSI-11/2 cards, and the PDP-11/03
computer system.

The F-11 was used in the LSI-11/23 card, and the PDP-11/23 (Qbus) and
PDP-11/24 (Unibus) computer systems.

The J-11 was used in the LSI-11/73 card, and the PDP-11/53, -11/73, -11/74,
-11/83, -11/84, -11/93, and -11/94 computer systems (3's are Qbus, 4's
are Unibus).

The J-11 was the first PDP-11 to have floating point (microcoded) as
standard; it also supported a hardware floating point accelerator.

The 11/44 and F-11 also supported the commercial instruction set (CIS)
as an option.

