In article <1992Mar4.190407.6612@iunet.ann-arbor.mi.us> ivars@iunet.ann-arbor.mi.us (Ivars Upatnieks) writes:


>> I have an Irwin 9131 external SCSI DAT drive I'd like to be able to use with
>> my DEC 5100.  I've talked with DEC, and they said that their implementation
>> of SCSI is standard, so from the hardware perspective this should be no 
>> problem.

>> On the other hand, from the software perspective, they said their SCSI tape
>> driver was especially designed to work with their tape drives.  They also
>> said that their drivers would not work with other tape drives.  

>> What are the chances that DEC's driver is standard enough that
>> I can plug in any SCSI tape drive and have it work?  Assuming DEC's drivers
>> will not work, where might I look for a third party driver that will?

>> ----
>> Ivars Upatnieks		emunix.emich.edu!ivars


Ivars,

 Yes, the SCSI Host Adapter implementation is compliant to an ANSI SCSI-2
interface.

 As for the DAT drive, if problems occur at this point, it's far more likely
to be the drives firmware implementation. You might ask the folks at Irwin
if they have a DEC compatible firmware version. A lot of vendors have never
brought their code into line with SCSI-2 and still have hold-overs from the
SCSI-1 specs (like not responding to a SCSI bus reset within 250ms). Some
vendors also have problems because they put things into their code for some
System vendor that had problems and now don't want to take it out because of
backwards compatibility problems.

 As far as the software goes, you might want to look at getting the SCSI CAM
iplementation put onto your DS5100. You can take a look at the documentation
(both the Guide and the Release Notes) which is available for anonymous ftp
on   gatekeeper: pub/DEC/TriAdd   and see if it will meet your needs (the
tape driver is pretty generic).

I hope that this helps.

