	This afternoon my attention was drawn to the cover story of this
weeks Digital News (DN), hyping the 'arrival' of DECs latest gamble, the
ALPHA architecture and some dramatic figures for the first chip, the
plug-replacement for a number of VAX systems in the 4000 and 6000 series,
and some NEWS about VMS apparently connected to the Alpha series:  VMS
will become OPEN-VMS (will this become a part of the "ULTRIX"[tm] family?),
they said, and source will be licensed to whoever wants it.  I'm para-
phrasing, here, I don't have the issue (let alone any of the 'real'
announcements) with me, didnt have the time to read any of this carefully,
and want to know more about the 64-bit ARCHitecture and how this is gonna
become an overnight plug-replacement standard for VAX.  Hah.  Sounds like
a good idea that should have been brought out 3 years ago.  Maybe DEC can
reclaim some ground, but after selling VAXes as the best thing since 
buttered toast for the last 15 years (just about) do they plan to put the
VAX Arch out to pasture like the 10/20?  Can they still hope for a future
for VAX like the old days when there was a different PDP processor for
different needs?  Is the VAX history?  Or is it all HYPE and the Alpha
is just the latest 'experiment' in marketing a successor to the dying
dinosaurs of yestermonth?

	I can certainly say I am pleased they intend to keep VMS a part
of the future, and I hope that VMS and Ultrix will become more alike,
seeing that until now you could run Ultrix on 2 architectures but VMS
only on a VAX platform.  Will Open-VMS become a successor to Ultrix,
then?  Or is that too much of a leap?

	[I think the Spring 1992 issue of D.T.J. will be on ALPHA...]


Having asked Enough questions for one night, i'll call it a night.


