In article <29B43CDA.13086@orion.oac.uci.edu>, fredv@rigel.acs.uci.edu (Fred Velijanian) writes:
> We are considering the purchase of a DECsystem 5500.
> We have access to four RA90 drives from a different
> configuration. How does the performance of the 
> DECsystem 5500 with RA90 drives, compare to 5500
> with SCSI drives (let's say RZ58s)?

	The KDA50 can run at around 1.15 MB/sec (max) and 700
	to 880 KB/sec sustained.  This information was taken
	from some Storage Systems glossies I have sitting
	around.  I'd guess that the 1.15 MB/sec is for one
	fast drive doing spiral transfers with multiple out-
	standing I/Os.  The lower number is probably for doing
	I/O with multiple drives.

	The SCSI adapter on the other hand claims to be able to
	do 4 MB/sec (no glossies to verify though).  Doing large 
	sequential I/Os on multiple RZ57s I've gotten it up to 
	around 2.4 MB/sec.  Better than the KDA50 in either case.

	Most interesting SCSI drives will have some sort of track
	or larger pre-fetch which will help with read performance.
	If you're write heavy though it might be wise to experiment
	with the rotational delay in the file system; if your
	application uses the file system.

	The next drive question is how many typical sized I/Os can
	it handle with reasonable response time using your applic-
	ations locality of reference; how many I/Os per second?
	What I/O size?  Average distance between seeks?  Very
	well tuned benchmarks can probably get reasonable response 
	at 50 transfers/sec. for single sectors I/Os.  For a very
	simple ULTRIX test I just ran (on a DS5810 with RA92s on an
	KDB50) I got around 25 tps using 8 KB I/Os.  That was over
	the entire range of the disk.

	I also ran similar test on RZ57s on the built-in SCSI
	adapter on a DS5500 and it was a little better.  Keep
	in mind that this was purely synchronous random reads.

> I know that RA90s
> have a lot going against them (old technology, expensive
> hardware maintenance, large footprint, ...), but do we
> gain "any" performance improvement by getting a 
> KDA50 controller and keeping the RA90s, vs buying 
> brand new SCSI drives? Our application is a database,
> i.e. heavily I/O bound. I am also worried that the 
> KDA50 controller and the RA drives are an "afterthought"
> on the DECsystems, and may not work as expected.

	The KDA50 is a five+ year old SDI disk controller.  Compared
	to the built-in SCSI or DSSI adapter on the I/O module of the
	5500 it has limited bandwidth and is way off on the other side
	of the Q-BUS interface.  The main reason for the support is
	for just the reason you asked; letting customers use existing
	drives.  It will work as expected.  You just have to know what
	to expect.

	Strictly from the stand point of performance, I don't think
	four RA90s sharing a KDA50 will perform better than similiar
	capacity SCSI disks on the built-in SCSI adapter for a common
	work-load.  Given time, I could probably construct a work-
	load where the RA90s would perform better.  As a guess I'd
	say it would be write heavy.

	If you are truly I/O bound, then what you want to do is
	spread the I/O load out between as many disk AND controllers
	as possible.  If the database will allow itself to be 
	configured across multiple disks, then putting part of it
	a couple of the RA90s and another part on some SCSI disks
	and maybe even another part on some DSSI disks may yield
	the best performance.
> 
> Thanks for any help
> 
> Fred Velijanian
-- 
Alan Rollow				alan@nabeth.cxo.dec.com



