In article <scs.699723496@hela.iti.org> scs@iti.org (Steve Simmons) writes:
>dfl@panix.com (Danny O'Bedlam) writes:
>A friend (who supposedly read it in the trades) says that the initial
>release of VMS on the alpha will be a 32-bit mode.  IMHO, anyone who
>buys today's Alpha chip thinking it'll run 64-bit tomorrow is taking
>the same risk that we all took thinking the 80286 would really run
>in two useful modes.  It might happen, but until you see running systems
>in 64-bit mode you'd best be cautious.

32-bit mode and 64-bit mode on the Alpha have nothing in common with the
dual modes of the 80286, 80386, and 80486 chips.  Those chips are best
compared to Intel's other great processor innovation, the 8008, which
was an 8-bit version of the 4004.  The 8080 was next, but a little
company called Zilog one-upped them and made a much better, more popular
version known as the Z80.  Intel then expanded the architecture to 16
bits with the 8086, and 8088.  The 286 was the first break from an
architecture based on a 4-bit processor, but it had emulation of the
8088, like the early VAXen emulated the PDP-11 (compatibility mode).

The Alpha chip has no such mode.  Instead, VMS will initially only allow
32-bit addresses.  This means that the upper 32 bits will be unused.
Actually, according to the hardware specifications, only 43 bits are
used internally, and 34 externally on the first chip.  That's enough for
16 GB of physical memory, which should be plenty for a while to come.
Future generations of the chip will likely expand on that even more.

>Don't get me wrong, I hope DEC succeeds wildly.  But I'm not betting
>the farm (or even the tractor) on it.

I'd be happy to bet the farm, personally.  The fact that they are
currently about 3 times ahead of the rest of the industry in terms of
clock-speed is amazing enough.  The architecture is a marvel of
engineering, specifically designed for multiple simultaneous instruction
execution.  And, Cray has already decided to use this chip in their next
massively parallel machine.

When Cray decides to use the chip, it's difficult to be skeptical.  This
may be what's needed to put Intel back in their place; the bottom of the
market (in terms of design, they've ben there for about 10 years now).

	Darrin
