							Jeff Killeen
							31 Hopedale St.
							Hopedale, Ma. 01747
							(508) 393-6299

							March 6, 1992

Dear fellow DECUS member,

	I am writing you this message because I believe you have a real
opportunity to change the way DECUS is guided by your choice in this Board
election.  Talk to almost anyone in leadership and you find agreement that the
U.S. Chapter is in need of change.  Today, however, there are two schools of
thought on this subject.  The prevailing school of thought has been that DECUS
needs to reform the structure of the Chapter - that if a structure is
developed that places the right leadership in firm control of the Chapter, all
will be well.  This school of thought led to the ADL $450,000 fiasco.  There
is a newer school of thought emerging that DECUS needs to reform its culture -
that the Chapter has developed a culture where leadership has become fixated
on itself and has developed the view that the right structure will resolve all
problems.  DECUS needs to change the culture from a leadership/structure focus
to having objective and fair input from the membership drive the Chapter's
decision process.  Your vote in this election could place DECUS firmly on one
road or the other. 

	I personally believe DECUS's problem is cultural.  No matter what
structure you develop, as long as the decision process is driven by leadership
opinion the Chapter will continue to experience endless and paralyzing debates
about whose opinion is correct.  Volunteer leadership cannot be driven no
matter how authoritarian a structure you create.  When the decision process is
driven by subjective personal opinion, volunteer leadership will correctly ask
why the driving force's opinion is any better their own.  This leads to
grid-lock and the foolishness the Chapter has seen over the last few years. 

	The culture that DECUS needs is one that seeks from the Chapter
membership objective input to drive the decision process. U.S. Chapter
leadership as a whole is very reasonable.  Give them provable real input as to
what the membership needs and they will do their best to make it happen.  Once
DECUS has a culture that drives a legitimate stake into the ground for all to
move towards, the Chapter will exhibit progress again. 

	The other part of the Chapter's culture that needs to be changed is
that objective input is needed from the membership at the end of the decision
process, not only at the beginning.  DECUS leadership needs to ask the
membership how it did when the Chapter does something.  It is only by asking
that DECUS will know if it drove the correct stake into the ground and if it
reached it. Only by asking can DECUS measure itself and improve itself. 

	I am asking you to vote for Dave Johnson, Emily Kitchen, and Sean
Welsh and I ask you to encourage others to do so.  I strongly believe that
Dave, Emily, and Sean have shown a leadership style that has a bias towards
letting objective membership input drive the decision process rather than
leadership's personal opinion.  If DECUS can elect enough of these types of
leaders to the Board over the next two years, I believe the culture itself
will change. 

	I firmly believe the other three candidates will lead DECUS down the
leadership focused road.  In fact, one of the candidates has called for a
structural change in both his ballot statement and at the LDEC forum your LUG
has on video tape. He uses the code words 'removing bureaucratic structures'
which are just a continuation of perceiving the structure rather than the
culture as the problem. The recommendation I am making is somewhat painful for
me because one of the candidates I am recommending you not vote for is one of
my best friends who I agree with on many issues.  Unfortunately I know his
management style will not help effect the cultural change DECUS needs.  So in
this election I am voting my conscience and not my politics. 

	You, the voter, should also beware of candidates who speak of talking
with the membership at DECUS events.  Experience and human nature has shown
they only tend to hear those voices that agree with their positions.  Randomly
interacting with membership is not a substitute for objective input from the
membership. 

	This needed cultural change will lead to DECUS becoming a customer
driven organization.  I believe this is as it should be.  What DECUS needs are
not leaders who feel they have all the answers but leaders who know they must
ask questions and respond to the answers. 


