In article <29B43B1D.12624@orion.oac.uci.edu>, fredv@rigel.acs.uci.edu (Fred Velijanian) writes:
|> I know that DECstations with different graphics boards
|> are not "supported" in a multi-headed configuration.
|> However I'd like to know if this "not supported" means that
|> the system won't work, or there are no guarantees as
|> to performance/funcionality of the system? Has anyone
|> tried running an "MX" and "HX"   or   "MX" and "PX" 
|> double headed system?  We have an extra MX card and
|> a VR319 that we'd like to use in one of our 5000/2xx 
|> which currently have HX or PX boards in them with VRT19s.

It's more of your "no guarantees as to performance/funcionality of the 
system" scenario.  There are two issues involved with mixing different
graphics options:

1)  Differing resolutions - There are two minor issues here, neither
of which is a big problem.  The X server only has one default font path, 
so if you have a 75 dpi screen and a 100 dpi screen, you only get to 
use either 100 or 75 dpi fonts as the default.  This is a minor 
cosmetic issue and you'll probably be happy with either.  The other
minor problem is that applications that try to be clever about 
screen geometry might get confused about a smaller or larger screen.  
(E.g. DECterm that remembers its geometry.)

2)  Different colormap models.  MIT X11R4 and prior releases of the 
X Window System have one set of application resources for the entire
workstation (display) and they're based on screen 0.  If the monochrome 
option is on screen 0, you may end up with monochrome on the HX, or if 
the color option is screen 0, you may end up trying to display colors on
the MX that obviously won't contrast.  You might end up with 
white-on-white colors, depending on how you customize things.  

If you're willing to experiment a little to come up with rational 
screen customizations (or just use the defaults) then I think you'll
be pretty safe.  

These restrictions about heterogeneous screens can be lifted someday as
MIT X11R5 contains screen-specific resource primitives.  This will allow
us to extend the resources files to do the right thing.

-joel


