	VMS will run in 32 bits and it will run faster than it does on
a VAX. The VMS market third parties have a different set of problems
from the UNIX third parties for Alpha. Obviously, we want to get Alpha
machines running VMS into their hands, so they can port thier software
to it. It's not a very productive approach to break everything and then
insist the third parties and customers fix it. We (DEC) are trying to
do the usual wire-walking routine - preserve user investment and make
things keep working, while providing a platform on which to springboard
into the future.

	The UNIX under Alpha crowd have it easier. Some code may break,
but most of it will just need a recompile. There's a lot less backwards
compatibility hacks to worry about - porting existing ULTRIX applications
to OSF/1 under MIPS and Alpha is a cakewalk - unless you were sloppy
and wrote code that assumed that sizeof(int) == sizeof(char *). It's
OK. The hardware informs you when you make that mistake. :)

	Backwards compatibility is like doing drugs - it hurts your
performance. If the backwards compatibility hacks are REAL good, then
nobody ever ports their applications and you're running PDP8 code
into the 21st century.

>The trouble is that many users take the path to mean it will work *well*
>even if DEC has disclaimed the shit out of it.  It seems quite unfair
>of the user community after such well known disclaimers.

