>  with DEC over the last 15 years : we will only listen to you through our
>  rigid SPR mechanism. I agree that it is probably best for you guys but
>  other people out there are eating your lunch as far as figuring out what
>  we, the customers, want and delivering it.

There is, IMHO, a good reason for the rigidity of the SPR mechanism.  It's
intended to make sure that by the time the person who's supposed to solve the
problem gets involved, that:
	1)  The alleged problem really exists;
	2)  The problem is well-defined.  This is the biggie.  If you follow
	    comp.os.vms at all closely, you'll have noticed that one of the
	    perennial problems with this newsgroup is that people post questions
	    of the form "This utility doesn't work in my application.  I've
	    checked my sysgen parameters, and still haven't solved the problem.
	    Which sysgen parameter do I have to change."  The initial question
	    frequently does NOT include either:
	    A)	Instructions for reproducing the problem; or
	    B)  The full text of the error messages being issued by the utility
		(perhaps we can blame UNIX for this;  it's taught users that
		error messages are meaningless);
	3)  That the problem is being routed to the appropriate group.
Now, trying to pry enough information out of some of the people who post
questions to this group that we have a chance of helping them is at times a
very difficult and frustrating task.  I, for one, believe that a customer has a
responsibility to provide 2A) and 2B) before expecting the vendor to help.  The
SPR system is set up in such a way as to try to ensure that they've done so
before DEC developers are asked to solve the problem.  I'd really rather not
have the developers spending countless hours prying the fact that, e.g., the
alleged "C compiler bug" is that it won't compile a FORTRAN program (as cc will
on a UNIX system).
