Brent,

  If I can jump in for a minute, I'd like to take a stab at what Gail is asking
for your support on. So, if you'll bear with my example...

  Say a company hires an engineer in to do development in diagnostic code for
hardware. That person's manager probably has an expectation that the engineer
is 100% committed in his job to the engineering project he was hired for. If
that engineer chooses to also try and help out with reading and responding to
questions in comp.sys.dec that is his own personal decision. If well intentioned
customers flood that engineer with private e-mail to the extent that it impacts
his diagnostic work then that person's manager is probably not going to be very
understanding.

  Your statement of "it OUGHT TO BE!" is sort of a "Quality is Job ONE" view.
I happen to agree with that view. And so do a lot of people in DEC, which is why
we do read and respond to these newsgroups (which is also why you see postings
from us at what seem like odd times of the day or night, or weekends or holidays
and on...). We do what we do because we believe in it, and sometimes we are also
lucky enough to have managers that believe in it (public acknowledgement here:
Brenda Christensen, who is my manager, knows about and supports my involvement
in these newsgroups).

  Nobody is looking for the customers to be "eternally grateful" for us being
involved in these forums. Just a little patience and understanding of workload
and response time. I try and answer all of the e-mail that gets sent to me. I
abjectly apologize for the delays that sometimes happen in responding to mail
that is sent to me. You will get answers, as rapidly as I can manage (I'm stil
just a uniprocessor remember ;-)...despite being schizophrenic I don't have full
SMP support, just a slaved system).

  What you can do to help make it the job of several people to read and respond
here is to send your requests to Gail so that she can use them as leverage to
get it funded and supported. I think it's a worthwhile effort.

Thanks for your time and patience.

				Thanx,
				  AJ


