Folks,

  I would tend to agree with this. My friend's problem reportedly stemmed
from the way she used to rest her wrists on the edge of the desk in front
of her keyboard. This type of positioning was also discouraged by the health
expert who was brought in to talk to us (she also made recommendations about
chair types and seated postions (which I shamefully admit to ignoring mostly,
I'm a real comfort adict...).

  Wrist rests were also made available to those who wanted them. I have been
using one on my LK201-RE for about 3 months now. I don't know if it's helping
(I hadn't noticed any fatigue previously either) but it did take some getting
used to. It's of roughly the same material as a mouse keypad, but it is about
0.75" in height. This brings my wrist height up to about half-way on the
space bar. I also use a key mod-map that puts the escape key and tilde where
I want them.

  In any case, if you find yourself feeling pain or numbness in your wrists
or fingers, please go see a physician immediately. You may feel foolish about
it, but that's a lot better than spending months in full wrist braces.

