Actually, the idea has a much longer vintage than that.  On the Ferranti
Atlas machines (first unveiled in 1958 - the first paged virtual memory 
machine, at least in this country), if the most significant bit of an 
instruction word was set, the instruction was interpreted as a look-up 
into the `extracode' table.  Extracodes were implemented in memory that
was then gee-whizz, but now (I suppose) totally outlandish technology, 
supposed to have _really fast_ access.  The main memory (all 48K words 
of it (? - or less!)) cycled, as I recall, at 6microseconds.

(Atlases never took off.  The real killer was losing an order in 
Australia to CDC.)

