	I recently saw a report on cable about a new style of "keyboard"
that's supposed to eliminate hand problems in typists.  I believe that the
unit was being developed at Arizona State University and was entering final
testing before being put to market. 

	(This thing is going to be really difficult to describe in words. 
If you don't get the gist, let me know and I'll try to explain it better.) 

	Basically, the new device is composed of two separate units; one
for each hand.  You place your palms on the form-fitting top of the units
so that your hands and wrists rest comfortably.  Your fingers and thumbs
then go into holes so that the tip of each finger is pointing straight down
like you're making a claw.  The holes contain sensor pads which record the
motion of the fingers in five directions; straight down, right, left,
forward, and back.  You type just by moving your fingers.  This avoids
injury and fatigue because your arms, wrists and hands are completely
supported and never move. 

	While this sounds really weird, they've actually used their heads 
and made it simple to learn.  The holes and sensors are set up so that the 
movements of the fingers are identical to those used on a standard QWERTY
keyboard.  In other words, you press your right index finger straight down 
to type a "j", forward to type a "u", left to type an "h",  etc.  Space and 
shift are handled by the thumbs, but I don't remember how they did the 
numbers.  According to the report, people who can already touch type are
able to acheive their normal typing speed on the new "keyboard" in just a
few hours of prectice. (Those of us who still look at the keys need a
little more time.)

	It looks like a good idea and not just some gimmick.  If anyone
knows more about it I'd really like to hear. 

						Dan Rohwer-Nutter
						dan@wystan.bsd.uchicago.edu
	


