	EEK! That's egregious! Have you tried actually watching what
your system is doing while this is happening? It is possible that there
is a side-effect of some configuration change that's hammering you?
	I'd try watching the server's I/O stats and so forth while
you're running this command from hell..

>Here is the dump command being run from the HP box:
>
>/etc/rdump 0udsf 250000 10000 - FS | remsh dumphost dd obs=10k of=/dev/nrmt0h

	A couple possibles. dd(1) in ULTRIX eats rocks. It's supposed to
be way faster because it uses N-buffered I/O, but in fact it *ALSO* does
character copies, and can clobber your CPU so badly the advantage of
N-buffered I/O is lost. I've never benched dd(1) and cat(2) - that'd be
an interesting comparison.

	If all you're using dd(1) for is to reblock data, then you should
use "reblock" or something like that which avoids the character copies.
If I were you, I'd try to use rdump the way it was intended - by letting
it talk to /etc/rmt. /etc/rmt handles blocking the tape properly and
does no character copies - it also (at least the BSD version) sets the
socket recv buffer and tries to make it large enough to fit the tape
blocksize, which can really help. (Oops. bleah, I just checked the
ULTRIX source. You might want to try FTPing the BSD rmt sources off
uunet. Dunno how much of a difference the recv buffer stuff makes.)

	Can you (if you have the time) give it a shot just talking to
plain old rmt rather than rshing it? If that does't help, drop me
email and we'll take a look at this offline.

mjr.
